/* Built by tools/build-split.py from data.js — do not edit. e1c, SDLs 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 */
(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.87d9f9d9ea.js"]={"14":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which brush border enzyme of duodenal epithelial cells first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Carboxypeptidase B","B":"Enteropeptidase","C":"Phospholipase A2","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Aminopeptidase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enteropeptidase, also called enterokinase, sits in the brush border glycocalyx of duodenal cells and cleaves a hexapeptide from trypsinogen to form trypsin. Carboxypeptidase B, phospholipase A2, and chymotrypsin are activated by trypsin afterward, and aminopeptidase is not the trigger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, enteropeptidase mediated trypsin activation, lines 2565–2569).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme on duodenal and jejunal mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: activation of pancreatic proteases begins in the duodenal lumen rather than in the gland (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pancreatic enzyme group is secreted already active rather than as a zymogen?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase A2","B":"Carboxypeptidase A","C":"Elastase","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Nucleases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases are secreted only as inactive proenzymes, while alpha amylase, lipases, cholesterol ester hydrolase, and nucleases are secreted active. Phospholipase A2, carboxypeptidase A, elastase, and chymotrypsin act only after their zymogens are cleaved by trypsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, synthesis and secretion of pancreatic enzymes, lines 2558–2564).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic alpha amylase is secreted in active form (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic enzymes are made on rough ER, pass through the Golgi, and are stored in apical zymogen granules (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which trypsin inhibitor do acinar and ductal cells package directly into zymogen granules?","choices":{"A":"PRSS1","B":"SPINK1","C":"CTRC","D":"SERPINA1","E":"MRP2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acinar and ductal cells co-package serine protease inhibitor Kazal type 1, SPINK1, into zymogen granules, where it inactivates up to 20 percent of prematurely formed trypsin. PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as a pancreatitis gene but is not the co-packaged inhibitor, SERPINA1 is not described here, CTRC aids trypsin self degradation, and MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin inhibitors, lines 2600–2603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations are causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: chronic pancreatitis is linked to alcohol use, tobacco use, cystic fibrosis, and hypertriglyceridemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","C":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","D":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","E":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as"}},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to about what bicarbonate concentration can stimulated pancreatic juice reach?","choices":{"A":"About 24 mEq/L","B":"About 70 mEq/L","C":"About 145 mEq/L","D":"About 300 mEq/L","E":"About 10 mEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells make an isotonic juice with bicarbonate up to about 140 to 145 mEq/L when stimulated. Plasma bicarbonate is about 24 mEq/L, and the other values are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, cellular mechanism of ductal HCO3 secretion and flow rate, lines 2612–2614, 2640–2643).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulated pancreatic juice is isotonic and alkaline (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, pancreatic juice has a reciprocal fall in chloride as bicarbonate rises (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, pancreatic duct cells move HCO3- across their apical membrane into the duct lumen. By which route do Na+ and water follow it to make an isotonic juice?","choices":{"A":"Through apical CFTR chloride channels","B":"Between cells through tight junctions","C":"Through apical sodium-hydrogen exchangers","D":"By apical sodium-potassium pumping","E":"By transcytotic vesicle transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Na+ and water follow HCO3- paracellularly across the tight junctions, down electrical and osmotic gradients, which makes pancreatic juice isotonic and alkaline. Apical CFTR channels carry Cl- back to the lumen to keep the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger running. The Na+/H+ exchanger and the Na+/K+-ATPase sit on the basolateral membrane, where they extrude H+ and acidify pancreatic venous blood. Transcytotic vesicle transport is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion and paracellular fluid secretion, p75).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice stay equal to plasma at all flow rates (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, HCO3- rises toward 145 mEq/L while Cl- falls (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — centroacinar and duct cells of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+/H+ exchanger and the N","D":"The Na+/H+ exchanger and the N"}},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how do Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice behave as flow rate changes?","choices":{"A":"Rise with flow","B":"Halve at low flow","C":"Exceed plasma twofold","D":"Stay equal to plasma","E":"Track chloride levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Na+ and K+ in pancreatic juice stay constant and equal to plasma at all flow rates, while HCO3- and Cl- change reciprocally. They do not rise, halve, double, or follow chloride. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2639).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at low basal flow, pancreatic juice is lower in HCO3 and higher in Cl- (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that low flow between meals lowers the HCO3 to Cl- ratio (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — ductal and centroacinar cells as the source of the bicarbonate-rich fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"They do not rise, halve, doubl"}},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the postprandial pancreatic enzyme response comes from the cephalic phase?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 80 percent","D":"About 35 percent","E":"About 20 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cephalic phase gives about 20 percent of the postprandial enzyme response, the gastric phase about 5 to 10 percent, and the intestinal phase about 70 to 80 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, phases of pancreatic secretion, lines 2658, 2668, 2674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimuli include sight, smell, taste, chewing, swallowing, and conditioned reflexes (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: the intestinal phase gives about 70 to 80 percent of the total pancreatic response (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary stimulus of the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Sight and smell of food","B":"Acidic duodenal chyme","C":"Stomach wall distention","D":"Duodenal fatty acids","E":"Rising blood glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mechanical distention of the stomach wall by food drives the gastric phase through long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits. Sight and smell drive the cephalic phase, acidic chyme and fatty acids drive the intestinal phase, and blood glucose is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase maintains a low volume, enzyme rich acinar secretion (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: gastric phase reflexes release ACh onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what mediator acts directly on acinar cells during the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Vagal acetylcholine","B":"Sympathetic norepinephrine","C":"Circulating secretin","D":"Circulating CCK","E":"Enteric VIP release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, central impulses travel in the vagus nerve and release ACh from postganglionic vagal endings directly onto acinar cells. Secretin and CCK are released later in the intestinal phase, and norepinephrine and VIP are not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase pathways, lines 2661–2663).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimulation yields a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK acts on acinar CCK1 receptors and through vagovagal reflexes in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Secretin and CCK are released "}},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the solutes in bile are bile salts?","choices":{"A":"About 4 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 40 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts make up about 50 percent of bile solutes, phospholipids mainly lecithin about 40 percent, cholesterol about 4 percent, and bile pigments about 2 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, bile formation and composition, lines 2695–2697).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes produce bile continuously, about 0.5 to 1.0 L per day (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile is composed of bile salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, bilirubin, water, and ions (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — the bile salt, phospholipid, and cholesterol balance behind cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocyte enzyme converts cholesterol into the primary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"HMG CoA reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Lipoprotein lipase","D":"7alpha hydroxylase","E":"Acyl CoA synthetase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes make cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid from cholesterol through 7alpha hydroxylase, the rate limiting enzyme. HMG CoA reductase makes cholesterol, heme oxygenase breaks down heme, lipoprotein lipase acts on plasma triglycerides, and acyl CoA synthetase activates fatty acids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, primary bile acids, lines 2702–2703).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7alpha hydroxylase catalyzes the rate limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: the primary bile acids are cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants and cholesterol conversion into bile acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what change do intestinal bacteria make to primary bile acids to form secondary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroxylation at C7","B":"Conjugation with glycine","C":"Glucuronidation at C3","D":"Oxidation at C12","E":"Sulfation at C24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids at C7, turning cholic acid into deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid into lithocholic acid. Conjugation with glycine or taurine happens in the liver. Glucuronidation is how the liver conjugates bilirubin, and glucuronidation, C12 oxidation, and C24 sulfation would all add groups, whereas deoxycholic and lithocholic acid each lack the C7 hydroxyl of their parent acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, secondary bile acids, lines 2704–2706).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the secondary bile acids are deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: bacteria in the ileum and colon deconjugate and modify bile salts to form secondary bile acids (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — ursodiol, a secondary bile acid used as a bile acid agent","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of secreted bile salts pass on to the terminal ileum after lipid absorption?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After lipid digestion and absorption in the jejunum, about 95 percent of secreted bile salts pass down to the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2758–2759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the terminal ileum is the site of bile salt reabsorption and vitamin B12 absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, small intestine). COMLEX: reabsorbed bile salts return to the liver in portal blood for re-secretion into bile (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which carrier moves bile salts from the ileal lumen into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bile salt cotransporter","B":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","C":"Proton peptide cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Multidrug resistance protein 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal enterocytes take up bile salts actively through Na+ bile salt cotransporters, called ASBT. Sodium glucose and proton peptide cotransport carry sugars and peptides, the chloride bicarbonate exchanger moves anions, and MRP2 exports conjugated bilirubin into bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2760–2761).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces reuptake at the terminal ileum into the portal circulation and back to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: hepatocytes extract returning bile salts efficiently through basolateral carriers (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Sodium glucose and proton pept"}},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how large is the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"About 25 to 30 g","B":"About 2.5 to 3 g","C":"About 0.2 to 0.3 g","D":"About 12 to 15 g","E":"About 100 g"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bile salt pool is about 2.5 to 3.0 g and recirculates several times per meal, for a daily flux of 12 to 30 g. The 25 to 30 g and 12 to 15 g values fall within the daily flux range rather than the pool, 100 g is far too large, and 0.2 to 0.3 g is too small. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: returning bile salts inhibit the rate limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids in the gut lumen to prevent bile acid diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid sequestrants).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of bilirubin comes from the breakdown of aging red blood cells?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 65 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages in the spleen, liver, and bone marrow, and about 15 percent from hepatic hemoproteins such as P450 cytochromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2780–2783).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBC destruction releases hemoglobin, which yields heme for bilirubin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism). COMLEX: unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and travels to the liver bound to albumin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which macrophage enzyme converts heme to biliverdin?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Glucuronyl transferase","D":"Beta glucuronidase","E":"Ferrochelatase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Macrophage heme oxygenase turns heme into biliverdin, which biliverdin reductase then reduces to unconjugated bilirubin. Glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin in hepatocytes, bacterial beta glucuronidase deconjugates it in the gut, and ferrochelatase builds heme. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2784–2786).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliverdin is green and bilirubin is yellow brown (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin). COMLEX: indirect bilirubin is unconjugated and water insoluble (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporter moves conjugated bilirubin across the canalicular membrane into bile?","choices":{"A":"MRP2","B":"ASBT","C":"OATP","D":"CFTR","E":"NTCP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conjugated bilirubin is actively pumped across the canalicular membrane by MRP2, multidrug resistant protein 2. ASBT takes up bile salts in the ileum, CFTR is a chloride channel, and OATP and NTCP are basolateral uptake carriers not described for this step. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and excretion, lines 2797–2799).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dubin Johnson syndrome is a defective MRP2 transporter with reduced excretion of conjugated bilirubin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: Dubin Johnson and Rotor syndromes are excretion defects (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has acute pancreatitis after a small amount of trypsin is activated inside the gland. Which built in property of trypsin normally limits this damage?","choices":{"A":"Autolysis aided by CTRC","B":"Acinar cell apoptosis","C":"Bicarbonate neutralization","D":"Lysosomal colocalization","E":"Bile salt inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trypsinogen and trypsin carry cleavage sites that allow autocatalytic self degradation, helped by chymotrypsin C or caldecrin, if trypsin is activated early. Apoptosis, bicarbonate, and bile salts do not inactivate trypsin, and lysosomal colocalization is not described as protective. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin self inactivation, lines 2604–2607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zymogens stay unactivated until they leave the ducts and meet enteropeptidase in the small intestine (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: chronic alcohol use and tobacco use are listed causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis asks why her enzyme capsules need more than lipase. Her physician explains that duodenal bile salts inhibit lipase on its own. Which cofactor, activated by trypsin, binds lipase so it can still work?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lecithin","C":"Albumin","D":"Colipase","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trypsin converts procolipase to colipase, which binds pancreatic lipase and lets it digest lipid in the presence of inhibitory bile salts. Elastase is a protease, lecithin is a phospholipid, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and secretin is a hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, cascade activation by trypsin, lines 2578–2580).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides into 2 monoglycerides and free fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: trypsin also activates prophospholipase A2 (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with meals. Which capsule protease removes single amino acids from the C terminal end of peptides?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic trypsin","B":"Pancreatic chymotrypsin","C":"Pancreatic elastase","D":"Brush border aminopeptidase","E":"Carboxypeptidase B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carboxypeptidases A and B are exopeptidases that remove single amino acids one at a time from the C terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase are endopeptidases that cut internal bonds, and aminopeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than a pancreatic capsule protease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, digestive functions of activated enzymes, lines 2582–2585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsin also cleaves more trypsinogen into active trypsin in a positive feedback loop (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic alpha amylase produces disaccharides and trisaccharides from starch and glycogen (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase for exocrine insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has poor pancreatic bicarbonate output. Normally, how does her defective apical channel support bicarbonate secretion?","choices":{"A":"Pumping bicarbonate into blood","B":"Secreting enzymes into acini","C":"Absorbing sodium from the lumen","D":"Recycling chloride to the lumen","E":"Generating carbonic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical CFTR Cl- channel lets Cl- recycle back into the lumen, keeping luminal Cl- high enough for continuous Cl-/HCO3- exchange. CFTR does not pump bicarbonate into blood, secrete enzymes, absorb sodium, or make carbonic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, role of CFTR Cl- channels, lines 2623–2627).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis is a listed cause of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology). COMLEX: secretin raises duct cell cAMP to open CFTR and drive Cl-/HCO3 exchange (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as a cause of chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. As his duct cells secrete bicarbonate, how does the venous blood leaving his pancreas change?","choices":{"A":"It becomes more alkaline","B":"It stays unchanged","C":"It loses chloride","D":"It gains bile salts","E":"It becomes more acidic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The H+ formed with each HCO3- is pumped across the basolateral membrane by Na+/H+ exchange into capillaries, acidifying pancreatic venous blood. It does not become alkaline or stay unchanged, and chloride and bile salts are not the described change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion, lines 2628–2631).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sodium and water follow HCO3 paracellularly across tight junctions into the duct lumen (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes pancreatic fluid as isotonic, with water, bicarbonate, and electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic fluid).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin and CCK regulation of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has pancreatic juice collected during a secretin test, and her flow rises sharply. How does the anion content of the juice change?","choices":{"A":"Chloride up and bicarbonate down","B":"Bicarbonate up and chloride down","C":"Both anions rise together","D":"Neither anion changes","E":"Bicarbonate down and chloride flat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At high flow, centroacinar and ductal cells secrete large volumes with bicarbonate rising to about 140 to 145 mEq/L and a reciprocal fall in chloride. Low flow shows the opposite pattern, and the two anions change in opposite directions rather than together, not at all, or with one held flat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2644).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: ACh and CCK potentiate secretin driven bicarbonate secretion through IP3 and Ca2+ (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Low flow shows the opposite pa","D":"Low flow shows the opposite pa"}},{"id":"sdl14_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a prior truncal vagotomy joins a pancreatic function study. A balloon distends his stomach while no chyme is allowed to enter the duodenum, and his pancreatic enzyme output still rises slightly. Which pathway most likely carries this signal?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent fibers to acinar cells","B":"Secretin from duodenal S cells","C":"Local enteric neural circuits","D":"Sympathetic splanchnic fibers","E":"Cortical conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastric distension drives the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion through two routes: long vagovagal reflexes and local enteric neural circuits, both of which release ACh onto acinar cells. With the vagal trunks divided, the local enteric circuits are the route left to carry the signal. Vagal efferent fibers were cut by the vagotomy. Secretin release needs acid chyme below pH 4.5 in the duodenum, which was prevented. Sympathetic fibers do not drive acinar enzyme secretion. Cortical conditioned reflexes belong to the cephalic phase and travel in the vagus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase pathways, p76).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase makes a low-volume, enzyme-rich juice and provides about 5 to 10 percent of the enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: sympathetic input generally inhibits GI secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman smells lunch cooking, and her pancreas secretes enzymes that mostly stay in the duct system until later. Why do they stay there?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition by somatostatin","B":"Very low fluid volume","C":"Ductal reabsorption of enzymes","D":"Absent zymogen granules","E":"Low enzyme synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cephalic response is a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water or electrolyte volume, so most enzymes stay stored in the ducts until fluid flow increases later. Somatostatin, ductal enzyme reabsorption, absent granules, and low synthesis are not the described reason. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase secretory response, lines 2664–2667).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cephalic phase, ACh is released from postganglionic vagal endings onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits secretin and CCK release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagal parasympathetic innervation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man eats a large meal, and chyme distends his duodenum and excites stretch and chemical receptors in the duodenal wall. Which pathway then amplifies the effects of secretin and CCK?","choices":{"A":"Vagovagal reflexes","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Gastrocolic reflex","D":"Sympathetic reflexes","E":"Myenteric peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the intestinal phase, distention and chemical stimuli excite vagal afferents and trigger efferent ACh release, a vagovagal reflex that potentiates both secretin and CCK. The enterogastric and gastrocolic reflexes, sympathetic reflexes, and peristalsis are not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, intestinal phase mechanisms, lines 2686–2688).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal phase stimuli include acidic chyme and fat and protein digestion products (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK stimulates massive zymogen exocytosis from acinar cells in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her bile salts can work in the duodenum. They stay ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH. Which feature explains this?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial dehydroxylation","B":"Albumin binding in bile","C":"Low pKa after conjugation","D":"Micelle core packing","E":"Bicarbonate buffering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa from about 7 to between 1 and 4, so bile salts are fully ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH (3 to 5 in the lecture). Bacterial dehydroxylation makes secondary bile acids, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and micelles and buffering do not set the pKa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, conjugation into bile salts, lines 2707–2711).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acids are conjugated in the liver with glycine or taurine to form amphipathic bile salts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: conjugation with glycine or taurine makes bile acids water soluble (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his bile salts break large fat globules into tiny emulsified droplets. By up to about how much does the lipid surface area increase?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 times","B":"Up to 2 times","C":"Up to 100 times","D":"Up to 1 million times","E":"Up to 1000 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bile salts lower interfacial tension, and intestinal mixing breaks fat into droplets under 1 µm, raising surface area up to 1000 fold for pancreatic lipase. Their negative charges repel and keep the droplets from merging. Two, 10, or 100 times understates the gain, and 1 million times overstates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in emulsification, lines 2716–2721).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states that emulsification raises surface area for pancreatic enzymes and increases fat digestion and absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid function). COMLEX: bile salts have a hydrophobic steroid nucleus and hydrophilic negatively charged groups (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase restoring fat digestion when lipase is deficient","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Two, 10, or 100 times understa"}},{"id":"sdl14_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman digests a fatty meal, and mixed micelles carry lipids across the unstirred water layer to her enterocytes. Where do the bile salts end up once the lipids enter the cells?","choices":{"A":"In the gut lumen","B":"Inside the enterocyte","C":"In the portal blood","D":"In the lymph lacteals","E":"In the brush border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the brush border, monoglycerides and fatty acids leave the micelle and diffuse into enterocytes, while bile salts are left behind in the lumen for later ileal reabsorption. They do not enter the jejunal enterocyte, portal blood, lacteals, or brush border with the lipids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2730–2733).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile functions include absorption of lipids and fat soluble vitamins (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: bile is the main route for eliminating cholesterol and bilirubin (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation returns bile salts from the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"They do not enter the jejunal ","E":"They do not enter the jejunal "}},{"id":"sdl14_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease has his terminal ileum resected and develops steatorrhea even though his liver now makes more bile acids. Why does steatorrhea occur?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis is shut off","B":"Synthesis cannot match losses","C":"Lipase is destroyed in bile","D":"Micelles form too easily","E":"Gallbladder stops storing bile"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Less bile salt returns to the liver, which relieves inhibition and raises synthesis, but even maximal synthesis falls behind heavy fecal loss. The pool shrinks, lipid solubilization fails, and steatorrhea follows. Synthesis rises rather than stopping; micelles form too poorly, not too easily, and the cause is the shrunken bile salt pool rather than lipase destruction or loss of gallbladder storage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2770–2774).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malabsorption of bile salts at the distal ileum, as in Crohn disease, prevents normal fat absorption (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: ileal bile salt loss lets dietary calcium bind fat, so free oxalate is absorbed and oxalate stones form (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Synthesis rises rather than st"}},{"id":"sdl14_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman fasts overnight, and her liver bile flows into her gallbladder for storage. What are the states of her sphincter of Oddi and gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Open with gallbladder contracted","B":"Open with gallbladder relaxed","C":"Closed with gallbladder relaxed","D":"Closed with gallbladder contracted","E":"Spasming with ductal reflux"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed and the gallbladder is relaxed, so continuously made bile flows into the gallbladder. After a meal CCK reverses both, contracting the gallbladder and relaxing the sphincter. Spasm with ductal reflux is not the fasting state; the closed sphincter simply diverts bile into the relaxed gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, gallbladder storage, lines 2738–2740).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi so bile is ejected in pulsatile spurts (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: vagal ACh adds to CCK in stimulating gallbladder emptying (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After a meal CCK reverses both"}},{"id":"sdl14_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man eats three meals a day. About how many times does his bile salt pool recirculate with each meal?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"10 to 12 times","C":"Once per day","D":"5 to 6 times","E":"20 to 30 times"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile salt pool of about 2.5 to 3.0 g recirculates 2 to 3 times per meal, for 12 to 30 g per day. Once per day is too few, and the larger counts are too many. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95 percent of bile salts reach the terminal ileum for reuptake (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: portal blood carries returning bile salts back to hepatocytes (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Once per day is too few, and t","D":"Once per day is too few, and t","E":"Once per day is too few, and t"}},{"id":"sdl14_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with sickle cell disease has jaundice during a hemolytic crisis. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Strongly positive urine bilirubin","B":"Absent urine urobilinogen","C":"Pale clay colored stools","D":"Negative urine bilirubin","E":"Raised conjugated bilirubin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemolytic jaundice raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, which is albumin bound and is not filtered by glomeruli, so urine bilirubin is negative. Urine urobilinogen rises and stools are dark. Strong urine bilirubin, absent urobilinogen, and pale stools fit obstruction, and conjugated bilirubin stays normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, prehepatic jaundice, lines 2813–2823).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemolysis is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice). COMLEX: jaundice appears when total serum bilirubin exceeds about 2.0 to 2.5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Urine urobilinogen rises and s"}},{"id":"sdl14_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a pancreatic head mass that completely blocks her common bile duct. Which stool and urine pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Dark stools with clear urine","B":"Dark stools and raised urobilinogen","C":"Pale stools and dark urine","D":"Normal stools and normal urine","E":"Green stools and red urine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut, so stools are pale and urine urobilinogen is absent, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin spills into urine and makes it dark and tea colored. Dark stools and raised urobilinogen fit hemolysis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, posthepatic jaundice, lines 2835–2846).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder is the Courvoisier sign (First Aid 2025 GI biliary structures). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that about 80 percent of urobilinogen becomes stercobilin for fecal excretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that appears during fasting. Which hepatocyte enzyme of the smooth ER is mildly deficient?","choices":{"A":"HMOX1","B":"UGT1A1","C":"BLVRA","D":"CYP7A1","E":"CYP2E1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome is an inherited mild UGT1A1 deficiency that lowers bilirubin glucuronidation in the smooth ER and raises unconjugated bilirubin. HMOX1 makes biliverdin, BLVRA makes bilirubin, CYP7A1 is the rate limiting 7 alpha hydroxylase of bile acid synthesis, and CYP2E1 is induced by alcohol, so none of them conjugates bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and hepatic jaundice, lines 2793–2796, 2824–2827).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crigler Najjar type I has severely reduced UGT1A1 activity and type II less severe loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: phenobarbital raises UGT1A1 expression and may help Crigler Najjar type II (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with a fatty meal. When phospholipase A2, activated by trypsin, cleaves a fatty acid from dietary phospholipid, what product remains?","choices":{"A":"Monoglycerides","B":"Free cholesterol","C":"Lecithin","D":"Lysolecithin","E":"Glycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2 cleaves fatty acids from phospholipids to yield lysolecithin. Monoglycerides come from lipase acting on triglycerides, free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase, lecithin is the starting phospholipid, and glycerol is not the described product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, lipolytic enzymes, lines 2588–2592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol ester hydrolase converts cholesterol esters to free cholesterol and fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: endopeptidases such as trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase cleave internal peptide bonds (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. Besides making it inside the cell, by which basolateral route do his duct cells take up extra bicarbonate from blood?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate symport","B":"Chloride bicarbonate exchange","C":"Sodium proton exchange","D":"Paracellular diffusion","E":"Aquaporin water channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Extra HCO3- enters the ductal cell across the basolateral membrane by Na+/HCO3- cotransport. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger sits on the apical side, Na+/H+ exchange removes H+ basolaterally, sodium and water move paracellularly into the lumen, and aquaporins carry water. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, carbonic anhydrase activity, lines 2618–2619).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+ made with HCO3 leaves duct cells by basolateral Na+/H+ exchange and Na+/K+ ATPase (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: secretin is the primary stimulant of aqueous HCO3 secretion (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman eats lunch. During the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion, what kind of secretion do her acinar cells make?","choices":{"A":"High volume bicarbonate fluid","B":"Low volume enzyme juice","C":"Pure electrolyte secretion","D":"Mucus rich ductal secretion","E":"Bile rich duodenal reflux"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gastric phase uses long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits that release ACh onto acinar cells and keep up a low volume, enzyme rich secretion. High volume bicarbonate fluid is the secretin response of the intestinal phase. The other choices are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase gives about 5 to 10 percent of the postprandial enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: intestinal phase amino acid triggers include phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man recovering from a cholecystectomy absorbs fat normally after a fatty meal. About what size are the mixed micelles that carry his lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"30 to 60 nm","B":"3 to 6 µm","C":"3 to 6 nm","D":"300 to 600 nm","E":"0.3 to 0.6 mm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mixed micelles are cylindrical complexes about 3 to 6 nm across, made of bile salts with lecithin, fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, and lysolecithin. Emulsified droplets are larger, under 1 µm, and the other sizes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2723–2726).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a micelle, hydrophobic lipids sit inside and the charged polar groups of bile salts face outward (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: negative charges on surface bile salts keep emulsified droplets from merging again (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has gallstones, and her biliary pain begins after a fatty meal when duodenal I cells release their hormone. Besides contracting her gallbladder, what else does this hormone do?","choices":{"A":"Sphincter of Oddi relaxation","B":"Sphincter of Oddi contraction","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Ileocecal valve closure","E":"Gallbladder wall relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CCK from I cells contracts the gallbladder wall and at the same time relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so concentrated bile spurts into the duodenum. It does not contract the sphincter of Oddi or relax the gallbladder wall, and the pylorus and ileocecal valve are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, CCK mediated gallbladder ejection, lines 2748–2756).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction against an obstructing stone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic). COMLEX: CCK is released when fatty acids, monoglycerides, and amino acids enter the proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It does not contract the sphin","C":"It does not contract the sphin","D":"It does not contract the sphin"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man on long courses of broad spectrum antibiotics notices lighter stools. Normally, which bacterial product gives stool its brown color?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin","B":"Bilirubin","C":"Urobilinogen","D":"Hemosiderin","E":"Stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic bacteria oxidize most urobilinogen to stercobilin and urobilin, which give feces their normal dark brown color. Biliverdin is the green heme breakdown product, bilirubin is deconjugated and reduced in the gut, urobilinogen is colorless, and hemosiderin is a tissue iron store. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, intestinal metabolism and excretion, lines 2800–2805).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 20 percent of urobilinogen is reabsorbed and a small share reaches the urine as yellow urobilin (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bacterial beta glucuronidases deconjugate bilirubin in the terminal ileum and colon (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"In acute and chronic hepatitis, what does Robbins call the pyknotic eosinophilic remnants of apoptotic hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Mallory hyaline","B":"Acidophil bodies","C":"Russell bodies","D":"Ground glass cells","E":"Psammoma bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Irreversible hepatocyte injury by apoptosis leaves pyknotic, eosinophilic acidophil bodies ringed by mononuclear cells. Robbins notes that these were first described in yellow fever and named Councilman bodies, and by convention keeps that eponym for yellow fever; in other hepatitis the term is acidophil bodies. Mallory hyaline is clumped intermediate filament in ballooned cells, ground glass cells mark chronic hepatitis B, and Russell and psammoma bodies are not liver apoptosis findings. Sources: Robbins ch 18, hepatocyte apoptosis (SDL 15–19 course reading, p. 6); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatocyte responses to injury, lines 2850–2856).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp and the lecture use Councilman bodies more loosely, for the eosinophilic inclusions of acute viral hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute viral hepatitis); expect either name. COMLEX: confluent necrosis is zonal loss of contiguous hepatocytes, such as zone 3 perivenular necrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cell is mainly responsible for collagen deposition and scar formation in the injured liver?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Resident Kupffer cells","C":"Portal tract fibroblasts","D":"Ductal cholangiocytes","E":"Sinusoidal endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatic stellate or Ito cells are the main collagen depositing cells. Cytokines such as TNF alpha and TGF beta from Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes activate them, and they become myofibroblasts that lay down type I and III collagen in the space of Disse. Kupffer cells signal but do not make the scar. Portal fibroblasts may also contribute in some disorders, but stellate cells are the principal source; sinusoidal endothelium loses its fenestrations as the collagen is laid down rather than making it, and cholangiocytes are not named as scar forming cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, scar formation and hepatic stellate cells, lines 2857–2865); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal fibroblasts may also play a role in scarring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: collagen in the space of Disse causes loss of endothelial fenestrations, called sinusoidal capillarization (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: PDGF drives proliferation of activated stellate cells (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kupffer cells signal but do no","C":"Portal fibroblasts may also co","D":"Portal fibroblasts may also co"}},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy arising within what time of the first injury?","choices":{"A":"Within 2 weeks","B":"Within 52 weeks","C":"Within 6 weeks","D":"Within 26 weeks","E":"Within 5 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute liver failure is acute liver illness with encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury in a patient without preexisting liver disease, and it shows massive necrosis with a small shrunken liver. The other windows are not the lecture definition. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, acute liver failure, lines 2873–2876).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute liver failure, portal hypertension develops over days to weeks and shows as ascites (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid lists drug injury such as acetaminophen, ischemic hepatitis, viral hepatitis, and autoimmune hepatitis as causes of very high aminotransferases (First Aid 2025 GI liver markers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what genome and structure does hepatitis A virus have?","choices":{"A":"Enveloped partially double stranded DNA","B":"Enveloped positive sense RNA","C":"Nonenveloped positive sense RNA","D":"Enveloped circular defective RNA","E":"Nonenveloped negative sense RNA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HAV is a small nonenveloped single stranded positive sense RNA picornavirus. Partially double stranded DNA is HBV, enveloped positive sense RNA is HCV, enveloped circular defective RNA is HDV, and no nonenveloped hepatotropic virus is negative sense. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis A virus, lines 2898–2901).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG anti HAV persists for life and confers long term immunity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: HAV spreads by the fecal oral route and does not cause chronic liver disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how long after exposure do anti-HCV antibodies emerge in hepatitis C infection?","choices":{"A":"3 to 6 days","B":"3 to 6 weeks","C":"1 to 3 weeks","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"6 to 12 months"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hepatitis C, HCV RNA becomes detectable within 1 to 3 weeks of infection along with elevated aminotransferases, and anti-HCV antibodies emerge 3 to 6 weeks after exposure. One to 3 weeks is the timing of HCV RNA, not antibody. Three to 6 days is too early for either marker. Three to 6 months and 6 to 12 months are too late; persistent HCV RNA beyond 6 months instead defines chronic infection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C serological markers and timing, lines 2943–2946).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists HBV and HCV as the viral hepatitides classically associated with hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists HCV among the causes of cirrhosis linked to hepatocellular carcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"One to 3 weeks is the timing o"}},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker is crucial for diagnosing acute hepatitis B after HBsAg clears but before anti HBs appears?","choices":{"A":"IgG anti HBs","B":"IgG anti HBc","C":"IgM anti HAV","D":"IgG anti HDV","E":"IgM anti HBc"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the window period after HBsAg clears and before anti HBs appears, IgM anti HBc identifies acute infection. IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark past or resolved infection, and the HAV and HDV antibodies belong to other viruses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2921–2926).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBeAg and HBV DNA indicate active replication and high infectivity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HBs rises after resolution and is the basis of the hepatitis B vaccine (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark"}},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which organisms most commonly ascend the biliary tree to cause cholangitis and pyogenic liver abscess?","choices":{"A":"Enteric coliforms","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Group A streptococci","D":"Anaerobic clostridia","E":"Pseudomonas species"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric bacteria, most commonly coliforms such as E. coli along with enterococci, ascend the obstructed biliary tree and can extend into the liver to form pyogenic abscesses. Staphylococcus aureus, group A streptococci, clostridia, and Pseudomonas are not the gut organisms the lecture names (E. coli, enterococci, Klebsiella) as ascending the obstructed tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis pathogenesis, lines 2989–2992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Charcot triad of cholangitis is jaundice, fever, and right upper quadrant pain (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis). COMLEX: the Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock to the Charcot triad (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what morphologic finding characterizes cholestasis of sepsis?","choices":{"A":"Zone 3 necrosis","B":"Interface hepatitis","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Ductular bile plugs","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholestasis of sepsis shows ductular or cholangiolar cholestasis, with dark bile plugs in dilated canals of Hering and ductules at the portal parenchymal interface and little inflammation. Zone 3 necrosis, interface hepatitis, ground glass cells, and onion skin fibrosis are other patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, sepsis and cholestasis of sepsis, lines 2998–3005).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sepsis injures the liver by direct bacterial action, hypoperfusion, or the host response to circulating endotoxin (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: complications of ascending cholangitis include sepsis and intrahepatic abscess (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which imaging feature is characteristic of hydatid cysts in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Air fluid level","B":"Central stellate scar","C":"Peripheral target sign","D":"Septated hemorrhage","E":"Calcified cyst wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Echinococcus larval tapeworms form large hydatid cysts that characteristically show calcification of the cyst wall on imaging. A central stellate scar suggests focal nodular hyperplasia. An air fluid level, a peripheral target sign, and septated hemorrhage are not described for hydatid disease; Robbins notes the wall calcification is what may enable radiologic diagnosis, and First Aid calls it eggshell calcification. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, echinococcosis, lines 3016–3019); Robbins Ch. 18 (hydatid cyst wall calcification); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Echinococcus: eggshell calcification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydatid cysts carry a risk of rupture, anaphylaxis, and daughter cyst spread (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: liver flukes inhabit intrahepatic bile ducts and cause hepatolithiasis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which autoantibodies characterize type 1 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"LKM1 and LC1","B":"AMA and gp210","C":"ANA and ASMA","D":"ANCA and MPO","E":"Anti CCP and RF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear and anti smooth muscle actin antibodies, sometimes with anti SLA or LPA, and high IgG. LKM1 and LC1 mark type 2. AMA points to primary biliary cholangitis, and ANCA and anti CCP fit other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3048–3052).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists hypergammaglobulinemia with raised IgG in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is linked to thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and celiac disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which enzyme is the target of LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP2E1","C":"CYP3A4","D":"CYP1A2","E":"CYP2C9"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis are directed against cytochrome P450 CYP2D6. CYP2E1 makes NAPQI from acetaminophen, and CYP3A4, CYP1A2, and CYP2C9 are not described as autoantigens here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3056–3058).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that autoimmune hepatitis is often positive for anti smooth muscle or anti LKM1 antibodies (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 2 autoimmune hepatitis mainly affects children and adolescents (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of autoimmune hepatitis patients are female?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 78 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent, with a genetic tie to HLA alleles such as HLA DR3 in Europeans. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3027–3031).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltration with periportal piecemeal necrosis in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: autoimmune hepatitis can progress to acute liver failure, cirrhosis, or hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cytochrome converts excess acetaminophen into its toxic metabolite?","choices":{"A":"CYP3A4","B":"CYP2D6","C":"CYP2E1","D":"CYP2C9","E":"CYP1A2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After an overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation, acetaminophen is shunted to CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI. CYP3A4, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, and CYP1A2 are not the isoform the lecture names for NAPQI formation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3086).","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction of CYP2E1 by chronic alcohol or anticonvulsants increases acetaminophen susceptibility (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists zone 3 centrilobular necrosis in acetaminophen toxicity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature describes idiosyncratic drug induced hepatotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Highly predictable","B":"Not dose dependent","C":"Reproducible in animals","D":"Immediate after exposure","E":"Seen in most users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity is unpredictable, not dose dependent, occurs in only a small minority of exposed people, and has variable latency. Predictable, reproducible, rapid injury is direct intrinsic toxicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, general pathogenesis and categories, lines 3067–3074).","boardPrep":"Step 1: idiosyncratic susceptibility comes from polymorphisms in drug metabolizing enzymes, transporters, or HLA alleles (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: direct hepatotoxicity is predictable, dose dependent, and occurs shortly after exposure (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Predictable, reproducible, rap"}},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug is listed as a cause of microvesicular steatosis from mitochondrial toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Amiodarone","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Isoniazid","D":"Oxaliplatin","E":"Valproate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, and nucleoside analogs such as zidovudine cause microvesicular steatosis. Amiodarone causes steatohepatitis, methotrexate fibrosis, isoniazid acute hepatitis, and oxaliplatin sinusoidal obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3120).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvesicular steatosis presents with liver dysfunction, hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and encephalopathy without overt necrosis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: methotrexate, retinoids, and enalapril are listed causes of periportal or pericellular fibrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has new confusion and a nonrhythmic flapping of his outstretched wrists. Which mechanism best explains these signs?","choices":{"A":"Low serum albumin","B":"Raised blood ammonia","C":"High serum bilirubin","D":"Low platelet count","E":"High portal pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic encephalopathy, with confusion and asterixis, comes from raised blood ammonia when hepatic urea cycle detoxification fails. Low albumin, high bilirubin, low platelets, and portal pressure do not cause asterixis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatic encephalopathy, lines 2877–2881).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis as neurologic signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ammonia with high PT or INR and low albumin in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic liver failure from long standing cholestasis has severe, constant itching. What most likely causes her itching?","choices":{"A":"Histamine from mast cells","B":"High serum ammonia","C":"Excess estrogen","D":"Low clotting factors","E":"Retained bile salts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent cholestasis in chronic liver failure causes profound pruritus from systemic buildup of retained bile salts and solutes. Ammonia causes encephalopathy, excess estrogen causes spider angiomas and gynecomastia, low clotting factors cause bleeding, and mast cell histamine is not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure and pruritus, lines 2889–2892).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, conjunctival icterus, pruritus, and fetor hepaticus in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists jaundice among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis has gynecomastia, palmar erythema, and spider angiomas on his chest. Which change best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Excess testosterone output","B":"Raised serum ammonia levels","C":"Impaired estrogen clearance","D":"Portal venous congestion","E":"Reduced clotting factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired hepatic estrogen clearance causes palmar erythema, spider angiomas, hypogonadism, and gynecomastia. Testosterone is not raised, ammonia causes encephalopathy, portal congestion causes ascites and varices, and low clotting factors cause bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure, lines 2892–2894).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists gynecomastia, hypogonadism, anovulation, and palmar erythema in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists spider angiomas and palmar erythema among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man still has detectable HBsAg 8 months after an acute hepatitis illness. Which state does this finding define?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis window period","B":"Resolved infection","C":"Acute HDV coinfection","D":"Chronic hepatitis B","E":"Prior HBV vaccination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistence of HBsAg beyond 6 months defines chronic HBV infection. The window period has no HBsAg, resolved infection and vaccination show anti HBs without HBsAg, and HDV coinfection is usually a self limited acute illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2927–2929).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, about 5 to 10 percent of HBV infections progress to chronic hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: perinatal HBV transmission leads to chronic infection in about 90 percent of infants (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is 30 weeks pregnant develops severe acute hepatitis during a waterborne outbreak in South Asia. Which virus is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis E","B":"Hepatitis A","C":"Hepatitis B","D":"Hepatitis C","E":"Hepatitis D"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HEV spreads by the fecal oral route in waterborne epidemics, and acute HEV in pregnant women carries a mortality approaching 20 percent. HAV is also fecal oral, but the lecture ties this pregnancy mortality to HEV, and HBV, HCV, and HDV are parenteral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis E virus, lines 2975–2978).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HEV is a nonenveloped positive sense RNA virus in the Hepeviridae family (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: chronic HEV occurs only in immunocompromised hosts such as organ transplant recipients (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — liver disease in pregnancy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","C":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","D":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","E":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th"}},{"id":"sdl15_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man who is a long standing HBsAg carrier and injects drugs develops a new acute hepatitis, and IgM anti HDV is positive. What outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Rapid HBsAg clearance","B":"Lifelong HDV immunity","C":"Mild self limited illness","D":"Clearance of both viruses","E":"Chronic HDV infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HDV superinfection of a chronic HBsAg carrier leads to chronic HDV in more than 80 percent of cases, with rapid progression to severe hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC. Coinfection with HBV and HDV at the same time is the usually self limited pattern. Lifelong HDV immunity and clearance of both viruses are unlikely, because the carrier's persistent HBsAg supplies the envelope HDV needs, and anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis D virus, lines 2960–2964).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HDV encodes a single internal polypeptide, the delta antigen (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lifelong HDV immunity and clea"}},{"id":"sdl15_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice, and enteric bacteria grow from her bile. Which condition sets the stage for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Biliary obstruction","C":"Right heart failure","D":"Alcohol overuse","E":"Hepatic artery spasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrahepatic or intrahepatic biliary obstruction from stones, strictures, or tumors creates stagnant bile that lets bacteria multiply and ascend, causing ascending cholangitis. Portal thrombosis, heart failure, alcohol, and arterial spasm are not the described setup. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis cause and risk factors, lines 2985–2988).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp explains that biliary obstruction impairs the mechanisms that keep bacteria out of the biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists conjunctival icterus, pale stools, and dark urine in acute cholangitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man from Southeast Asia often eats undercooked freshwater fish and has flukes in his intrahepatic bile ducts. Which tumor is he at greatly increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Gallbladder adenoma","C":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","D":"Pancreatic cystadenoma","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opisthorchis and Clonorchis flukes live in intrahepatic bile ducts and cause chronic biliary inflammation, hepatolithiasis, and a dramatically increased risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Flukes raise the risk of bile duct cancer rather than hepatocellular carcinoma, gallbladder adenoma, or pancreatic cystadenoma, and hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, liver flukes, lines 3012–3015); Robbins Ch. 18 (angiosarcoma: vinyl chloride, arsenic, Thorotrast).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Clonorchis sinensis infection as a risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists liver fluke infection such as Clonorchis as a cholangiocarcinoma risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI biliary tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — HCC versus other hepatic malignancies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has bloody dysentery and weeks later a liver abscess that drains thick anchovy paste fluid. Which organism most likely caused it?","choices":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Echinococcus granulosus","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Clonorchis sinensis","E":"Giardia lamblia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites breach the colonic mucosa, causing amebic dysentery, and travel by portal blood to form liver abscesses full of necrotic anchovy paste debris. Echinococcus forms hydatid cysts, Schistosoma causes portal fibrosis, Clonorchis lives in bile ducts, and Giardia does not invade. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, amebiasis, lines 3020–3023).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebiasis spreads by the fecal oral route (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: bacteria can also reach the liver by portal hematogenous spread or direct extension (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — portal venous flow that carries gut organisms to the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with thyroiditis has chronic liver inflammation with high IgG and circulating autoantibodies. Which histologic feature is most characteristic of her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Onion skin fibrosis","B":"Florid duct lesion","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Zone 3 necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows prominent interface hepatitis, dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates with plasma cell clusters, emperipolesis, and hepatocyte rosettes. Onion skin fibrosis fits PSC, the florid duct lesion fits PBC, ground glass cells fit chronic HBV, and zone 3 necrosis fits toxic or ischemic injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, morphologic features, lines 3036–3042).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emperipolesis is intact lymphocytes or plasma cells engulfed within hepatocytes (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid describes a portal and periportal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in autoimmune hepatitis (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has autoimmune hepatitis with anti LKM1 antibodies and no ANA. Which other autoantibody may also be present?","choices":{"A":"Anti mitochondrial","B":"Anti liver cytosol 1","C":"Anti smooth muscle","D":"Anti neutrophil cytoplasm","E":"Anti tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows anti LKM1 and or anti liver cytosol 1 antibodies, with ANA and smooth muscle antibodies typically absent. Anti mitochondrial antibodies fit PBC, anti smooth muscle fits type 1, and the other antibodies fit vasculitis and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3054–3059).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ANA and anti smooth muscle in type 1 and anti LKM1 in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: serum IgG is elevated in both types of autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Anti mitochondrial antibodies ","E":"Anti mitochondrial antibodies "}},{"id":"sdl15_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman of European descent develops autoimmune hepatitis after a course of minocycline. Which HLA allele is most linked to her disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA B27","B":"HLA DQ2","C":"HLA DR3","D":"HLA B51","E":"HLA DR2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis is linked to HLA DR3 in Europeans, HLA DR4 in Japanese, and HLA DRB1 in South Americans, and drugs such as minocycline are proposed triggers. HLA B27, DQ2, B51, and DR2 are tied to other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3029–3033).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proposed triggers of autoimmune hepatitis include viral infections, drugs, and vaccinations (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: CD4 helper T cells drive B cells to make autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions and characteristic serum antibodies of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman takes a large acetaminophen overdose and two days later has very high aminotransferases. Depletion of which protective molecule allows the toxic metabolite to injure her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Glutathione","B":"Glucuronic acid","C":"Sulfate stores","D":"Cytochrome P450","E":"Bilirubin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess NAPQI depletes glutathione and then binds cellular proteins, damaging mitochondria and killing zone 3 hepatocytes. Glucuronidation and sulfation are the normal pathways that are saturated, cytochrome P450 makes NAPQI, and bilirubin is not protective here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3089).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetaminophen is the most common cause of acute liver failure requiring transplantation in the United States (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that depleted glutathione raises NAPQI and causes hepatocyte necrosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Glucuronidation and sulfation "}},{"id":"sdl15_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man had high dose chemotherapy for a bone marrow transplant and now has tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, ascites, and rising bilirubin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Budd Chiari syndrome","B":"Portal vein thrombosis","C":"Veno occlusive disease","D":"Acute viral hepatitis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, or veno occlusive disease, follows high dose cytotoxic drugs, with toxic sinusoidal endothelial injury and fibrous obliteration of terminal hepatic venules. Budd Chiari involves the large hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis mainly causes abdominal pain and portal hypertension with varices, and drug induced bland cholestasis presents with pruritus, jaundice, and a high alkaline phosphatase rather than tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, and ascites after transplant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, lines 3137–3146); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal vein obstruction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrrolizidine alkaloids in bush tea also cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: azathioprine, oxaliplatin, and cyclophosphamide are listed causes of sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man taking isoniazid for latent tuberculosis develops fever, rash, eosinophilia, and jaundice with ALT more than 5 times normal. Which injury pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Microvesicular steatosis","B":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","C":"Bland canalicular cholestasis","D":"Acute inflammatory hepatitis","E":"Sinusoidal endothelial injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isoniazid, nitrofurantoin, phenytoin, methyldopa, and checkpoint inhibitors cause idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation, fever, rash, eosinophilia, and ALT at least 5 times normal. Microvesicular steatosis follows valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, or zidovudine; bland canalicular cholestasis follows oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids; sinusoidal endothelial injury follows high dose cytotoxic chemotherapy; and zone 3 confluent necrosis is the acetaminophen pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute inflammatory hepatitis, lines 3108–3116).","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune checkpoint inhibitors are listed causes of idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes eosinophilia with halothane induced liver injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, inhaled anesthetic toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with acute liver failure has easy bruising and a prolonged prothrombin time. What is the main cause of her bleeding tendency?","choices":{"A":"Low clotting factor output","B":"Platelet antibody destruction","C":"Dietary vitamin C deficiency","D":"Excess heparin release","E":"Bone marrow aplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Coagulopathy in liver failure comes from defective hepatic synthesis of clotting factors II, V, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, and fibrinogen and from failure to clear activated factors. Platelet antibodies, vitamin C deficiency, heparin, and marrow aplasia are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, coagulopathy, lines 2882–2885).","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver failure coagulopathy predisposes to easy bruising, severe bleeding, and DIC (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR and low platelets in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — complications of alcohol-associated liver disease and MASLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man who injects drugs has chronic hepatitis C and starts a drug aimed at the viral RNA dependent RNA polymerase. Which viral protein does it target?","choices":{"A":"NS3","B":"NS5A","C":"NS5B","D":"NS4A","E":"Core"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NS5B is the HCV RNA dependent RNA polymerase, and its high error rate creates genotypes and quasispecies. NS3 and NS4A form the protease, NS5A is part of the replication complex, and core is structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C viral proteins, lines 2938–2942).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80 to 90 percent of HCV infections become chronic, and about 20 percent progress to cirrhosis over 20 to 30 years (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HCV antibodies appear 3 to 6 weeks after exposure, and HCV RNA appears within 1 to 3 weeks (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who swam in freshwater lakes in Africa as a child now has portal hypertension and portal fibrosis without cirrhosis. Which infection is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Clonorchiasis","B":"Echinococcosis","C":"Amebic abscess","D":"Schistosomiasis","E":"Hepatic fascioliasis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Schistosoma spreads through freshwater with snail vectors. Adult worms live in mesenteric veins, and eggs lodge in portal venules, causing granulomas, portal fibrosis, and noncirrhotic portal hypertension. Clonorchiasis affects bile ducts, echinococcosis forms cysts, amebiasis forms abscesses, and fascioliasis is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, schistosomiasis, lines 3007–3011).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cirrhosis and schistosomiasis as separate intrahepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid lists schistosomiasis among causes of portal hypertension (First Aid 2025 GI portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and the portosystemic collaterals that form varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has autoimmune hepatitis, and her biopsy shows a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate. Which cells work with CD4 helper T cells to kill the hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil granulocytes","B":"Activated mast cells","C":"Resident Kupffer cells","D":"Natural killer cells","E":"CD8 cytotoxic T cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In autoimmune hepatitis, CD4 helper T cells activate B cells to make autoantibodies, while CD8 cytotoxic T cells work with CD4 cells to attack hepatocytes at the interface. Neutrophils, mast cells, Kupffer cells, and NK cells are not the described effectors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3033–3035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocyte rosettes are regenerating hepatocytes arranged around dilated canaliculi (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid notes autoimmune hepatitis may present with fatigue, nausea, or pruritus (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Neutrophils, mast cells, Kupff"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy is given aspirin for influenza and days later has vomiting, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. Which liver finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Macrovesicular steatosis","B":"Microvesicular steatosis","C":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","D":"Portal interface hepatitis","E":"Pericellular bridging fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High dose aspirin in children causes Reye syndrome, a mitochondrial toxicity with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus. Macrovesicular fat pushes the nucleus aside, zone 3 necrosis fits acetaminophen, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune disease, and bridging fibrosis is chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3126).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes Reye syndrome as mitochondrial injury with hyperammonemia, encephalopathy, and cerebral edema (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Reye syndrome). COMLEX: First Aid links Reye syndrome to aspirin given for viral infections such as VZV and influenza (First Aid 2025 GI Reye syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long standing hepatitis C has a liver biopsy showing fibrous septa that encircle nodules of surviving hepatocytes. What does this pattern define?","choices":{"A":"Cirrhosis","B":"Steatosis","C":"Cholestasis","D":"Hemosiderosis","E":"Amyloidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic persistent injury causes ongoing parenchymal loss and fibrous septa that encircle surviving hepatocytes, forming the regenerative nodules that define cirrhosis. Steatosis is fat, cholestasis is bile buildup, and hemosiderosis and amyloidosis are deposition diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, regeneration versus cirrhosis, lines 2866–2870).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after acute uncomplicated injury or partial hepatectomy, hepatocytes regenerate without scarring (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: if the injury stops, metalloproteinases can partly degrade fibrous bands (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true}],"16":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much daily alcohol intake for 10 to 20 years is the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease?","choices":{"A":"At least 8 g per day","B":"At least 20 g per day","C":"At least 200 g per day","D":"At least 80 g per day","E":"At least 40 g per day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture gives 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years as the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease, with women more susceptible at lower intake. Robbins (SDL 15-19 reading) puts it slightly differently: risk of severe injury becomes significant at 80 g or more per day, and 160 g or more per day for 10 to 20 years is frequently associated with severe injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, risk factors, lines 3149–3154).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists macrovesicular steatosis, neutrophils, ballooning, and Mallory Denk bodies in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds pericellular and sinusoidal fibrosis in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which proteins make up the Mallory hyaline seen in ballooned hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Keratin 8 and 18","B":"Desmin and vimentin","C":"Actin and myosin","D":"Tau and amyloid beta","E":"Collagen types I and III"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mallory hyaline, or Mallory Denk bodies, are tangled clumps of the intermediate filaments keratin 8 and 18 in ballooned hepatocytes. Desmin, vimentin, actin, myosin, tau, amyloid beta, and collagen are not its components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives an AST to ALT ratio above 1.5 to 1 with values under 500 U/L in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin with leukocytosis in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what AST to ALT ratio is typical of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease?","choices":{"A":"2 or greater","B":"About 1.5","C":"Less than 1","D":"Above 5","E":"Between 3 and 4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In MASLD the ALT usually exceeds the AST, so the AST to ALT ratio is less than 1. A ratio of 2 or more points to alcohol associated disease. Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, or above 5 all exceed 1, and the last two fall in the alcohol range. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives MASLD aminotransferases under 500 U/L with an AST to ALT ratio below 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists central obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high triglycerides, and low HDL as MASLD causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A ratio of 2 or more points to","B":"Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, o"}},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which HFE mutation is the most frequent cause of hereditary hemochromatosis?","choices":{"A":"H63D","B":"C282Y","C":"G551D","D":"F508del","E":"V600E"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is most often an autosomal recessive HFE mutation on chromosome 6, most frequently the C282Y cysteine to tyrosine change. H63D is a less frequent HFE variant, and the others are CFTR and BRAF mutations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hereditary hemochromatosis, lines 3185–3190).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Vibrio vulnificus, Yersinia enterocolitica, and Listeria as infections favored by iron overload (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists therapeutic phlebotomy, deferoxamine, deferasirox, and deferiprone as treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va","D":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va","E":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va"}},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are mutated in Wilson disease?","choices":{"A":"HFE on chromosome 6","B":"ATP7A on the X chromosome","C":"SERPINA1 on chromosome 14","D":"HAMP on chromosome 19","E":"ATP7B on chromosome 13"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive loss of function mutation in ATP7B on chromosome 13, a hepatocyte copper transporting ATPase. HFE causes hemochromatosis, SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency, HAMP encodes hepcidin, and ATP7A is a different copper transporter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3213–3217).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Fanconi syndrome as a complication of Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a low copper diet, penicillamine, trientine, and zinc salts as management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what circulating AAT level do PiZZ homozygotes have?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent of normal","B":"About 50 percent of normal","C":"About 30 percent of normal","D":"About 1 percent of normal","E":"About 75 percent of normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, and PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT levels only about 10 percent of normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3240).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes PAS positive, diastase resistant inclusions in periportal zone 1 hepatocytes in AAT deficiency (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture calls AAT deficiency the most common inherited liver disease diagnosed in infants and children (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what serum bilirubin level is typical of Crigler Najjar type I?","choices":{"A":"Below 3 mg/dL","B":"Above 20 mg/dL","C":"About 5 mg/dL","D":"Below 1 mg/dL","E":"About 10 mg/dL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type I causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically above 20 mg/dL. Gilbert syndrome is usually below 3 mg/dL, and type II is usually below 20 mg/dL. Values of about 5 or 10 mg/dL fit the moderate, usually below 20 mg/dL range of type II. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type I, lines 3258–3266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that phototherapy converts unconjugated bilirubin into a water soluble form excreted in urine and bile (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that liver transplant is curative in Crigler Najjar syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much normal UGT1A1 activity remains in Gilbert syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 3 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A1 expression to about 30 percent of normal activity, most often from a TATA box promoter mutation. Crigler Najjar type II keeps under 10 percent, and type I has none. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp calls Gilbert syndrome the most common inherited hyperbilirubinemia syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that Gilbert syndrome usually presents in adolescence with a mild course (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporters are deficient in Rotor syndrome?","choices":{"A":"MRP2 transporter","B":"UGT1A1 enzyme","C":"ATP7B transporter","D":"BSEP transporter","E":"OATP1B1 and 1B3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotor syndrome is an autosomal recessive deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, impairing hepatic reuptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin. MRP2 is defective in Dubin Johnson syndrome, and UGT1A1 in Gilbert and Crigler Najjar syndromes. ATP7B is the Wilson disease copper transporter, and BSEP exports bile salts rather than bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Rotor syndrome, lines 3294–3302); Robbins Ch. 18 (canalicular transporters: BSEP for bile salts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised total urinary coproporphyrin in Rotor syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Rotor syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that some conjugated bilirubin returns to the sinusoids and is taken up by downstream hepatocytes via OATP1B1 and 1B3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which laboratory pattern accompanies large bile duct obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Isolated raised AST","B":"Raised ALP and GGT","C":"Raised lipase and amylase","D":"Raised indirect bilirubin","E":"Raised CK and aldolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large bile duct obstruction raises serum alkaline phosphatase, GGT, and conjugated bilirubin, with jaundice, pruritus, dark urine, and pale stools. Indirect bilirubin, isolated AST, lipase, and muscle enzymes do not define it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes choledocholithiasis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds that the pain may radiate to the back or right scapula with nausea and diaphoresis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Indirect bilirubin, isolated A"}},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which nodule pattern marks biliary cirrhosis after uncorrected choledocholithiasis?","choices":{"A":"Garland shaped nodules","B":"Hobnail surface nodules","C":"Nutmeg mottled pattern","D":"Jigsaw puzzle nodules","E":"Onion skin layering"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected choledocholithiasis causes progressive periductal fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis with irregular jigsaw puzzle shaped regenerative nodules. Garland shaped cirrhosis is linked to PBC in the lecture, nutmeg liver is passive congestion, onion skin fibrosis is PSC, and a hobnail surface is not the lecture pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists surgical or ischemic strictures and PSC as adult causes of large duct obstruction (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists pancreatic head adenocarcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma as malignant adult causes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the morphologic hallmark of ascending cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas around ducts","B":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","C":"Plasma cell interface hepatitis","D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates","E":"Neutrophils in duct epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending cholangitis shows neutrophilic infiltration within bile duct epithelium and lumens. Granulomatous duct injury fits PBC, concentric fibrosis fits PSC, and plasma cell interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: severe ascending cholangitis can progress to pyogenic liver abscesses and Gram negative sepsis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists acute on chronic liver failure and death as outcomes of severe cases (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what fraction of PBC patients are women?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 70 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PBC has a striking female predilection, about 90 percent women at a 9 to 1 ratio, peaking at 40 to 50 years. PSC instead is about 70 percent male. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions and demographics, lines 3336–3344).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid and cholestyramine, then liver transplant, for PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hyperpigmentation, xanthomas, and hepatomegaly in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antigen do the antimitochondrial antibodies of PBC target?","choices":{"A":"PDC E2 subunit","B":"CYP2D6 enzyme","C":"Thyroid peroxidase","D":"Tissue transglutaminase","E":"Proteinase 3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AMA, present in 95 percent of PBC, targets the PDC E2 subunit of pyruvate dehydrogenase. CYP2D6 is the LKM1 target, and thyroid peroxidase, tissue transglutaminase, and proteinase 3 belong to other autoimmune diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists antimitochondrial antibodies with hypergammaglobulinemia in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a dense lymphocytic portal infiltrate with granulomas on PBC biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma in primary sclerosing cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 80 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PSC uniquely carries a high lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma of about 20 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3368–3372).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links PSC to ulcerative colitis, HLA B8, and HLA DR3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists supportive care then liver transplant as PSC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks a fifth of whiskey daily has AST 240 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L) and ALT 100 U/L (reference 10–40 U/L), and his CK is normal. Which mechanism best explains why his AST exceeds his ALT?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal AST clearance","B":"Bile duct leakage of ALT","C":"Selective hepatic ALT degradation","D":"Low hepatic pyridoxal phosphate","E":"Skeletal muscle AST release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alcohol associated disease the AST to ALT ratio is usually 2 or more because ethanol depletes hepatic pyridoxal 5 phosphate, and because ethanol injures hepatocyte mitochondria. Renal clearance, ductal leak, selective ALT degradation, and muscle release, which a normal CK argues against, are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR, low albumin, and low platelets in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists acute liver failure, cirrhosis, and HCC as complications (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Renal clearance, ductal leak, "}},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with central obesity and type 2 diabetes who does not drink has hepatomegaly and fatty liver on imaging. Which mechanism best explains the fat in her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Increased VLDL export","B":"More peripheral lipolysis","C":"Excess NADH from ethanol","D":"Reduced insulin resistance","E":"Copper driven radicals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In MASLD, insulin resistance increases lipolysis in adipose tissue, raising free fatty acid delivery to the liver, where triglyceride synthesis causes steatosis. VLDL export removes fat, ethanol derived NADH is the alcohol mechanism, and copper radicals fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, pathogenesis, lines 3155–3162).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes triglyceride and free fatty acid buildup causing ROS, mitochondrial dysfunction, and inflammation that progress to MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that MASLD is often asymptomatic apart from hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has a liver biopsy compared with that of a patient with MASH. Which finding is more prominent in his biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Mainly microvesicular fat","B":"Granulomatous duct injury","C":"Periportal iron granules","D":"Lymphoplasmacytic rosettes","E":"Abundant Mallory hyaline"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both diseases show steatosis, ballooning, and chicken wire pericellular fibrosis, but Mallory hyaline and neutrophilic infiltrates are more prominent in alcoholic hepatitis than in MASH. Fat is mainly macrovesicular, and the other findings belong to PBC, hemochromatosis, and autoimmune hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, anorexia, and fever in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hepatic encephalopathy in severe alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, ","C":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, ","D":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, "}},{"id":"sdl16_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has diabetes, slate gray skin, cardiomyopathy, and cirrhosis. His transferrin saturation is very high. Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Excess dietary heme iron","B":"Defective ceruloplasmin","C":"Blocked biliary iron excretion","D":"Low hepcidin synthesis","E":"Reduced ferroportin activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hereditary hemochromatosis, HFE mutations impair hepatic hepcidin synthesis. Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin uninhibited in enterocytes and macrophages, so iron is hyperabsorbed. Reduced ferroportin activity would lower absorption, and ceruloplasmin defects fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis pathogenesis, lines 3191–3196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation with low TIBC in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic transfusions and chronic liver disease as causes of secondary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has a new tremor, depression, and Coombs negative hemolytic anemia. His serum ceruloplasmin is low. Which eye finding is most likely on slit lamp exam?","choices":{"A":"Kayser Fleischer rings","B":"Cherry red macular spot","C":"Upward lens dislocation","D":"Blue tinted sclerae","E":"Conjunctival Bitot spots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wilson disease leaks free copper into the blood, which deposits in Descemet membrane at the corneal limbus as green to brown Kayser Fleischer rings. Cherry red spots, lens dislocation, blue sclerae, and Bitot spots fit other disorders. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3223–3236).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists low haptoglobin and raised indirect bilirubin with a negative direct Coombs test in Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low ceruloplasmin, raised 24 hour urine copper, and raised free serum copper (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has cholestatic hepatitis. His biopsy shows round cytoplasmic globules in periportal hepatocytes that stay pink after diastase digestion with PAS. Which mechanism best explains his liver injury?","choices":{"A":"Unchecked elastase in liver","B":"Copper buildup in cells","C":"Iron overload of cells","D":"Viral infection of cells","E":"Misfolded protein in ER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In AAT deficiency the PiZ protein misfolds and is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum, a toxic gain of function that triggers ER stress and hepatocyte death. Unchecked elastase injures the lungs, not the liver. Copper and iron fit Wilson disease and hemochromatosis. Viral infection does not produce PAS positive, diastase resistant globules; those are retained AAT. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3241–3254).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links abnormal AAT in hepatocytes to cell stress, inflammation, cirrhosis, and HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture gives a 2 to 3 percent HCC risk in adult AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl has unconjugated bilirubin of 28 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) that persists despite phototherapy. Her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal, and her direct Coombs test is negative. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Absent UGT1A1 activity","B":"Mildly reduced UGT1A1","C":"Defective MRP2 export","D":"Absent OATP1B1 reuptake","E":"Excess heme oxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Profound unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above 20 mg/dL with hemolysis excluded fits Crigler Najjar type I, a complete loss of UGT1A1 activity that risks kernicterus. Type II has residual activity that phenobarbital can induce. MRP2 and OATP defects raise conjugated bilirubin. Overproduction of bilirubin from heme, as in hemolysis, is excluded by the normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte count and the negative Coombs test. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar syndromes, lines 3258–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes persistent neonatal jaundice progressing to kernicterus in type I disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes type II is less likely to cause neonatal jaundice (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Type II has residual activity ","D":"MRP2 and OATP defects raise co"}},{"id":"sdl16_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has recurrent mild jaundice with conjugated bilirubin in her urine. Her liver biopsy shows coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes. Which hepatocyte transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoidal OATP1B1","B":"Basolateral NTCP","C":"Canalicular MRP2","D":"Canalicular BSEP","E":"Hepatocyte ATP7B"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with dark lysosomal pigment and a grossly black liver is Dubin Johnson syndrome, a loss of function of the canalicular MRP2 transporter. OATP1B1 loss causes Rotor syndrome with a normal looking liver, BSEP exports bile salts, NTCP takes up bile salts, and ATP7B defects cause Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Dubin Johnson syndrome, lines 3284–3302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists over 50 percent direct bilirubin with a mild course in Dubin Johnson syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes dark granular pigment in hepatic lysosomes on biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy notices mild yellow eyes after fasting and exam stress. His indirect bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and other tests are normal. Which genetic change most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Coding region frameshift","B":"Complete UGT1A1 deletion","C":"MRP2 nonsense mutation","D":"TATA box promoter mutation","E":"Biallelic OATP1B3 deletion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome causes mild, fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia under 3 mg/dL with stress or fasting, and is most often due to a TATA box promoter mutation that lowers UGT1A1 expression. Complete loss fits Crigler Najjar type I, and MRP2 or OATP changes raise conjugated bilirubin. A coding region frameshift that destroys the enzyme would fit complete loss of activity rather than Gilbert's reduced expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes some Gilbert syndrome variants are exceptions to autosomal recessive inheritance (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives Gilbert syndrome a normal life expectancy (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Complete loss fits Crigler Naj","E":"Complete loss fits Crigler Naj"}},{"id":"sdl16_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has jaundice, itching, tea colored urine, and clay colored stools from a blocked common bile duct. What best explains the color of her stools?","choices":{"A":"Raised fecal urobilinogen","B":"Excess biliverdin in gut","C":"Colonic bile salt deficiency","D":"Bacterial overgrowth in colon","E":"Absent intestinal stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With large duct obstruction, conjugated bilirubin fails to reach the gut, so no stercobilin forms and stools turn pale or acholic, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin darkens the urine. Fecal urobilinogen falls rather than rises, biliverdin is reduced to bilirubin in macrophages long before bile, and the brown color comes from bacterial stercobilin, so neither bile salt lack nor bacterial overgrowth explains pale stools. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, icterus, pale stools, dark urine, and pruritus in choledocholithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: the lecture explains that pruritus comes from systemic retention of bile salts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with a stone in her common bile duct develops epigastric pain radiating to the back and a lipase five times normal. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acute cholecystitis","B":"Acute pancreatitis","C":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","D":"Hepatic abscess","E":"Ascending cholangitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impacted ductal stones can cause secondary acute pancreatitis, shown here by back radiating epigastric pain and a high lipase. Cholangitis, abscess, cholecystitis, and ulcer perforation do not explain the lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists pressure necrosis and biliary colic from impacted ductal stones (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes choledocholithiasis as common bile duct obstruction by a gallstone with severe RUQ or epigastric pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with known gallstones has fever, chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice. Which named sign cluster does she show?","choices":{"A":"Beck triad","B":"Virchow triad","C":"Charcot triad","D":"Whipple triad","E":"Reynolds pentad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fever with chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice form the Charcot triad of ascending cholangitis, usually from enteric coliforms such as E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella. Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock, which she lacks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331); First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis (fa_gastro_cache 2267–2268).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture names E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella as the usual organisms of ascending cholangitis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes cholangitis as infection of a stagnant, obstructed biliary tree (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with autoimmune thyroiditis has fatigue, intense itching, and a high ALP. Her antimitochondrial antibody is positive. Which finding is most likely on liver biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Florid duct lesion","B":"Onion skin fibrosis","C":"Interface hepatitis","D":"Bile duct beading","E":"Ground glass cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, with the florid duct lesion of granulomatous, lymphoplasmacytic duct destruction. Onion skin fibrosis and beading fit PSC, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15, line 3036), and ground glass cells are not a PBC finding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, morphology and duct lesions, lines 3345–3351).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised cholesterol and triglycerides with markedly raised ALP in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp links PBC to autoimmune conditions such as Hashimoto thyroiditis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has a raised ALP on routine labs, and MRCP shows strictures alternating with dilations of his bile ducts. Which antibody is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Positive AMA","B":"Positive pANCA","C":"Positive ASMA","D":"Positive anti LKM1","E":"Positive anti dsDNA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PSC, linked to ulcerative colitis and showing a beaded biliary tree, is pANCA positive in about 65 percent, while AMA is characteristically negative. Anti smooth muscle and anti LKM1 fit autoimmune hepatitis, and anti dsDNA fits lupus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes concentric periductal onion skin fibrosis on PSC histopathology (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ERCP shows similar findings to MRCP and allows stent placement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Anti smooth muscle and anti LK"}},{"id":"sdl16_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with PBC for many years now has high serum cholesterol. Which skin finding is she most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Pretibial myxedema","B":"Malar rash","C":"Xanthelasmas","D":"Erythema nodosum","E":"Heliotrope rash"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PBC progresses over years to hypercholesterolemia, xanthelasmas, jaundice, steatorrhea, osteomalacia, and biliary cirrhosis. Pretibial myxedema fits Graves disease, the malar rash fits lupus, erythema nodosum fits sarcoidosis or IBD, and the heliotrope rash fits dermatomyositis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3364–3367).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists osteomalacia and steatorrhea late in PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes PSC as often found by a raised ALP during ulcerative colitis screening (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with obesity, hypertension, and type 2 diabetes has biopsy proven steatohepatitis without alcohol use. Beyond liver failure and cancer, which outcome is his disease additionally tied to?","choices":{"A":"Cardiovascular death","B":"Pulmonary embolism death","C":"Renal failure death","D":"Hemorrhagic stroke death","E":"Sepsis death"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MASLD and MASH progress like alcohol associated disease to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and HCC, and are additionally associated with high cardiovascular mortality from the underlying metabolic syndrome. The lecture ties no other extrahepatic cause of death to MASLD. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, complications, lines 3172–3175).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp names the inflammatory stage of MASLD metabolic dysfunction associated steatohepatitis, or MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin in MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with diabetes, bronze skin, and cirrhosis has a liver biopsy showing golden yellow granules in periportal hepatocytes. Which stain best highlights this pigment?","choices":{"A":"PAS with diastase","B":"Rhodanine copper","C":"Congo red","D":"Oil red O","E":"Prussian blue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hemosiderin in hereditary hemochromatosis starts in periportal hepatocytes and is highlighted intensely by Prussian blue iron stain, with no inflammation. PAS with diastase marks AAT globules, copper stains fit Wilson disease, Congo red marks amyloid, and oil red O marks fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis morphology, lines 3197–3203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists MCP 2 and 3 arthropathy and pseudogout in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists amenorrhea, hypogonadism, and loss of libido from iron deposition (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia near 12 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and residual UGT1A1 activity under 10 percent of normal. Which drug most likely lowers her bilirubin?","choices":{"A":"Penicillamine","B":"Cholestyramine","C":"Phenobarbital","D":"Phlebotomy","E":"Ursodeoxycholic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type II keeps residual UGT1A1 activity, and its jaundice responds to phenobarbital, which induces the residual enzyme. Penicillamine treats Wilson disease, phlebotomy treats hemochromatosis, and Bootcamp lists cholestyramine and ursodeoxycholic acid as PBC treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type II, lines 3267–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes conjugated bilirubin enters the bile canaliculi through MRP2 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives type II a milder course with low kernicterus risk (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has had three episodes of jaundice with right upper quadrant pain over the past year. Examination shows scleral icterus and a palpable right upper quadrant mass. Laboratory studies show a total bilirubin of 4.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL) and direct bilirubin of 3.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.0–0.3 mg/dL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and a large fusiform cystic dilation of the common bile duct. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Biliary atresia","B":"Choledochal cyst","C":"Cystic fibrosis","D":"Pancreatic pseudocyst","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lecture lists extrahepatic biliary atresia, choledochal cysts, and cystic fibrosis as the pediatric causes of large bile duct obstruction. A choledochal cyst is a congenital dilation of the common bile duct that usually presents before age 10 with jaundice, recurrent abdominal pain, and sometimes a mass, and a fusiform dilation of the duct with a normal gallbladder fits it. Biliary atresia presents in the first weeks of life with an absent or irregular gallbladder and progresses rather than coming and going. Cystic fibrosis obstructs ducts with thick secretions and does not form a cystic dilation of the common bile duct. A pancreatic pseudocyst follows pancreatitis and lies beside the pancreas rather than forming the duct wall. Crigler Najjar syndrome raises unconjugated bilirubin without duct dilation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, pediatric etiologies and clinical features, lines 3306–3318); Robbins Ch. 18 (choledochal cyst); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (biliary atresia ultrasound).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists biliary tract obstruction among the causes of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI, conjugated hyperbilirubinemia). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregularly shaped gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has fatigue, itching, a high ALP, and a positive antimitochondrial antibody. Which associated autoimmune condition is most common in her disease?","choices":{"A":"Sjögren syndrome","B":"Ulcerative colitis","C":"Crohn disease","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis","E":"Psoriatic arthritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is linked to Sjögren syndrome in about 70 percent, along with autoimmune thyroiditis, scleroderma, rheumatoid arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, and celiac disease. Ulcerative colitis goes with PSC. Ankylosing spondylitis is an HLA B27 disease, not one of PBC's autoimmune associations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions, lines 3336–3341); First Aid 2025 Immunology (HLA-B27: ankylosing spondylitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture notes that ANAs are present in 40 to 50 percent of PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture states PBC spares large intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old nonsmoking man has panacinar emphysema and cirrhosis. Both parents carry one abnormal copy of his mutated gene and have intermediate serum levels of its protein. Which term best describes how his alleles are expressed in serum protein levels?","choices":{"A":"Autosomal dominant","B":"X linked dominant","C":"X linked recessive","D":"Autosomal codominant","E":"Maternal mitochondrial"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, so each allele contributes protein and heterozygotes have intermediate levels. Robbins calls the disease autosomal recessive because clinical disease needs two Z alleles, but describes allele expression as codominant. Low circulating AAT lets elastase injure the lung, and retained PiZ protein injures the liver. Because SERPINA1 is autosomal and both parents carry it, X linked dominant and maternal mitochondrial inheritance do not fit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3246).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links SERPINA1 mutation to raised elastase activity with lung tissue destruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture states PAS positive globules start in periportal hepatocytes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Because SERPINA1 is autosomal "}}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, Budd Chiari syndrome is caused by thrombosis or obstruction of which veins?","choices":{"A":"One minor hepatic vein","B":"A single left hepatic vein","C":"The main portal vein","D":"Two or more major veins","E":"Scattered sublobular veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome is hepatic venous outflow obstruction from thrombosis or obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic problem, and a single small vein does not produce the syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome causes congestive liver disease with hepatomegaly, ascites, varices, and abdominal pain but no JVD (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and cardiac causes as posthepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis is a pr"}},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which disorder is a primary cause of hepatic vein thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Hemophilia A","B":"Polycythemia vera","C":"Von Willebrand disease","D":"Immune thrombocytopenia","E":"Vitamin K deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary causes of Budd Chiari syndrome include myeloproliferative neoplasms such as polycythemia vera, inherited thrombophilias, antiphospholipid syndrome, PNH, HCC invading hepatic veins, pregnancy, and oral contraceptives. The other choices are bleeding disorders, not thrombotic ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links Budd Chiari syndrome to hypercoagulable states, the postpartum state, and HCC (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: the lecture notes a swollen red purple liver with a tense capsule in Budd Chiari syndrome (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other choices are bleeding","C":"The other choices are bleeding","D":"The other choices are bleeding","E":"The other choices are bleeding"}},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the pericellular centrilobular fibrosis of long standing right heart failure called?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac sclerosis","B":"Bridging necrosis","C":"Hepatoportal sclerosis","D":"Chicken wire fibrosis","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long standing right heart failure leads to pericellular centrilobular fibrosis termed cardiac sclerosis. Bridging necrosis links lobules in severe hepatitis, hepatoportal sclerosis is a portal venous lesion, chicken wire fibrosis is the perisinusoidal pattern of steatohepatitis, and onion skin fibrosis surrounds ducts in PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3400).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists congestive hepatopathy and nutmeg liver with centrilobular necrosis as posthepatic findings (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists nonselective beta blockers such as carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol to prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and which other feature?","choices":{"A":"Jaundice","B":"Seizures","C":"Hypoglycemia","D":"Hematuria","E":"Hypothermia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Preeclampsia is maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects, and eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and seizures. Hypoglycemia is a feature of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Mild jaundice can occur in preeclampsia but does not define eclampsia, and hematuria and hypothermia are not listed features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia and eclampsia, lines 3405–3411).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preeclampsia can present as HELLP syndrome, with hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: the lecture explains preeclamptic liver injury by endothelial dysfunction, microvascular thrombosis, and vasospasm (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the characteristic histology of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Macrovesicular fat with ballooning","C":"Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis","D":"Portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate","E":"Diffuse microvesicular steatosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AFLP shows diffuse microvesicular steatosis, with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus that is not displaced. Periportal fibrin deposits fit preeclampsia, and ballooning with macrovesicular fat fits steatohepatitis. Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis is the ischemic or congestive zone 3 pattern, and a portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate fits idiosyncratic drug induced hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3428–3431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture gives AFLP an incidence of about 1 in 16,000 pregnancies (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe AFLP shows lobular disarray, hepatocyte dropout, and cholestasis (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which fetal enzyme defect underlies a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"LCHAD deficiency","B":"MCAD deficiency","C":"G6PD deficiency","D":"HGPRT deficiency","E":"CPT2 deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a subset of AFLP cases, homozygous fetal long chain 3 hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase deficiency lets toxic fatty acid intermediates cross into the mother and overwhelm maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. The other enzyme defects are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3427).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFLP requires urgent termination of pregnancy (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP presents late in pregnancy with bleeding, nausea, vomiting, jaundice, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostaining pattern is pathognomonic of focal nodular hyperplasia?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear beta catenin","B":"Loss of LFABP staining","C":"Map like GS staining","D":"CRP and SAA staining","E":"Membranous CD31 staining"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FNH shows a pathognomonic map like pattern of glutamine synthetase, or GS, staining. Nuclear beta catenin, LFABP loss, and CRP or SAA overexpression mark hepatocellular adenoma subtypes, and CD31 marks endothelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3444–3450).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls FNH the second most common benign liver tumor, mostly in women aged 35 to 50 years (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes FNH becomes isodense to the surrounding liver in the portal venous phase after arterial hyperenhancement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is most strongly associated with hepatocellular adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Vinyl chloride exposure","B":"Chronic hepatitis B","C":"Aflatoxin ingestion","D":"Liver fluke infection","E":"Oral contraceptive use"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign epithelial neoplasm strongly associated with oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, and metabolic syndrome or obesity. Vinyl chloride fits angiosarcoma, HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC, and liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3456).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes a hepatic adenoma may regress spontaneously or rupture with abdominal pain and shock (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp ties adenoma rupture to a rich arterial supply and lack of stroma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Vinyl chloride fits angiosarco","D":"Vinyl chloride fits angiosarco"}},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocellular adenoma subtype carries a high risk of transformation to HCC?","choices":{"A":"HNF1A inactivated","B":"Inflammatory subtype","C":"Sonic hedgehog subtype","D":"Beta catenin activated","E":"Fibrolamellar subtype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta catenin activated adenomas carry activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations, are common in males and anabolic steroid users, and have a high risk of HCC. HNF1A inactivated adenomas have minimal risk, inflammatory adenomas mild risk, and sonic hedgehog adenomas a high bleeding risk. Fibrolamellar is an HCC variant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection is indicated for adenomas in males, beta catenin mutants, or tumors 5 cm or larger (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: sonic hedgehog adenomas carry GLI1 fusions and a high risk of intraperitoneal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which precursor lesions give rise to HCC in cirrhotic livers?","choices":{"A":"Mallory bodies","B":"Dysplastic nodules","C":"Councilman bodies","D":"Hyperplastic polyps","E":"Florid duct lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCC arises from precursor lesions such as small cell change, large cell change, and dysplastic nodules in cirrhotic livers. Mallory and Councilman bodies are injury findings, florid duct lesions fit PBC, and hyperplastic polyps are colonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology and precursor lesions, lines 3482–3485).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls HCC the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults and notes hematogenous spread (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists tumor seeding during liver biopsy as an HCC complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene fusion drives fibrolamellar HCC?","choices":{"A":"TMPRSS2 ERG","B":"KIAA1549 BRAF","C":"DNAJB1 PRKACA","D":"ETV6 NTRK3","E":"RUNX1 RUNX1T1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fibrolamellar variant occurs in young adults without cirrhosis, is driven by a DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. The other fusions drive prostate cancer, pilocytic astrocytoma, infantile fibrosarcoma, and acute myeloid leukemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3489).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCC has a strong propensity for vascular invasion into the portal and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be green from cholestasis or yellow from fat (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other fusions drive prosta","B":"The other fusions drive prosta","D":"The other fusions drive prosta","E":"The other fusions drive prosta"}},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum tumor marker is raised in advanced HCC?","choices":{"A":"Carcinoembryonic antigen","B":"Cancer antigen 125","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Chromogranin A","E":"Alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Serum alpha fetoprotein is raised in advanced HCC, although it is unhelpful for early screening and negative in fibrolamellar HCC. CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogranin A mark other tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3495–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that HCC recurrence is monitored with serum AFP (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss, hepatomegaly, and abdominal pain in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogr","C":"CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogr"}},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is historically associated with hepatic angiosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Liver flukes","C":"Oral contraceptives","D":"Vinyl chloride","E":"Asbestos fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is historically associated with occupational vinyl chloride monomer, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Aflatoxin fits HCC, liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma, and oral contraceptives fit adenoma. Asbestos is linked to bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma rather than liver tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3527–3529); First Aid 2025 Pathology (carcinogens: asbestos, lung).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatic angiosarcoma is often metastatic at diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PECAM 1, or CD31, positivity in angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostains are positive in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"CK7 and CK19","B":"HepPar1 and AFP","C":"CD31 and CD34","D":"S100 and HMB45","E":"Desmin and actin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is positive for mucin and CK7 and CK19, and negative for AFP and HepPar1. CD31 and CD34 mark angiosarcoma, S100 and HMB45 mark melanoma, and desmin and actin mark muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3519–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid places the most common cholangiocarcinoma at the convergence of the right and left hepatic ducts (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which cells does hepatic angiosarcoma arise?","choices":{"A":"Intrahepatic biliary epithelium","B":"Perisinusoidal stellate cells","C":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells","D":"Polygonal hepatocytes","E":"Resident Kupffer macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is a rare, aggressive mesenchymal tumor of sinusoidal endothelial cells that forms hemorrhagic red purple masses. Biliary epithelium gives cholangiocarcinoma, and hepatocytes give HCC. Kupffer macrophages are not the cell of origin; the tumor stains for endothelial markers such as CD31, CD34, and ERG. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3526).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiosarcoma cells line and dissect along preexisting sinusoidal spaces (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: angiosarcoma stains for CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with antiphospholipid syndrome has abdominal pain, tender hepatomegaly, and new ascites. Doppler shows thrombi in three major hepatic veins. Which liver finding is most likely on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Portal egg granulomas","C":"Microvesicular steatosis","D":"Periductal onion skin","E":"Pericentral congestion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome causes marked pericentral or centrilobular sinusoidal congestion with hepatocellular necrosis, and chronic cases develop pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, egg granulomas fit schistosomiasis, microvesicular fat fits AFLP, and onion skin fibrosis fits PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome can produce a mottled nutmeg liver (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp links posthepatic obstruction to raised sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long standing right sided heart failure dies, and his liver is examined at autopsy. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Chocolate brown liver","B":"Diffusely green liver","C":"Nutmeg mottled liver","D":"Diffusely yellow liver","E":"Jigsaw nodular liver"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Passive congestion from right heart failure gives a nutmeg liver, a mottled red tan pattern of centrilobular congestion alternating with lighter periportal tissue. Chocolate brown fits hemochromatosis, green fits cholestasis, yellow fits steatosis, and jigsaw nodules fit biliary cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in passive congestion, raised central venous pressure is transmitted back through the IVC and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: centrilobular sinusoids are dilated and filled with red cells (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with severe cardiac decompensation has centrilobular hepatocyte necrosis on biopsy. Which hepatocytes are most likely to remain intact?","choices":{"A":"Zones 1 and 2","B":"Central zone 3","C":"Zones 2 and 3","D":"Zones 1 and 3","E":"Midzonal zone 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In passive congestion, centrilobular zone 3 hepatocytes undergo ischemic atrophy and necrosis while periportal zone 1 and 2 hepatocytes remain intact. Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, so any choice that includes zone 3 is wrong. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3395–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists paracentesis with a raised serum ascites albumin gradient in portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists TIPS as a portal hypertension treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s","C":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s","D":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s"}},{"id":"sdl17_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation has blood pressure of 162/110 mm Hg, proteinuria, edema, and right upper quadrant pain. Her AST is 310 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L). Which liver finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse microvesicular fat","B":"Pericentral congestion","C":"Portal plasma cell infiltrate","D":"Periportal fibrin deposits","E":"Bridging fibrous septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclampsia, periportal sinusoids contain fibrin deposits with hemorrhage into the space of Disse, leading to periportal coagulative necrosis. Microvesicular fat fits AFLP, pericentral congestion fits venous outflow problems, plasma cells fit autoimmune hepatitis, and septa fit chronic fibrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3412–3414).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with preeclamptic liver injury have raised transaminases and mild jaundice (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe coagulopathy marks advanced disease that needs prompt delivery (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman at 37 weeks of gestation with eclampsia suddenly develops severe right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic vein thrombosis","B":"Ruptured liver hematoma","C":"Acute cholecystitis","D":"Perforated peptic ulcer","E":"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In eclampsia, high pressure bleeding forms intrahepatic hematomas that can dissect beneath Glisson capsule into a subcapsular hematoma with a risk of catastrophic rupture. An ectopic pregnancy is not possible at 37 weeks of an intrauterine pregnancy. Hepatic vein thrombosis produces liver enlargement, pain, and ascites, acute cholecystitis begins with progressive pain lasting more than 6 hours with mild fever, and a perforated ulcer causes peritonitis with free air, so none fits sudden pain with shock in eclampsia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3414–3417); Robbins Ch. 18 (Budd-Chiari syndrome; acute calculous cholecystitis clinical features); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, perforation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture defines HELLP as hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: preeclampsia combines maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman at 35 weeks of gestation has nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and confusion. Her glucose is 45 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), and viral serologies are negative. Which mechanism best explains her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Maternal autoimmune hepatitis","B":"Acute hepatitis A infection","C":"Hepatic microvascular thrombosis","D":"Canalicular bile salt retention","E":"Overwhelmed beta oxidation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy presents in the third trimester with incipient liver failure, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. In a subset, fetal LCHAD deficiency sends toxic fatty acid intermediates into the mother, overwhelming maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. Microvascular thrombosis is the preeclampsia mechanism. Canalicular bile salt retention is cholestasis, which brings pruritus and a high alkaline phosphatase rather than hypoglycemia and encephalopathy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3435).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in AFLP, tiny fat droplets surround a central nucleus without displacing it (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP is a rare, life threatening metabolic emergency of the third trimester (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a 4 cm liver mass found incidentally on imaging. It is unencapsulated with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Urgent surgical resection","B":"Conservative management","C":"Hepatic artery embolization","D":"Systemic chemotherapy","E":"Liver transplantation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A circumscribed unencapsulated nodule with a central stellate scar is focal nodular hyperplasia, a benign hyperplastic lesion with no risk of malignant transformation or spontaneous rupture, so it is usually managed conservatively. Resection, embolization, chemotherapy, and transplant are reserved for lesions such as adenomas 5 cm or larger or malignancy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FNH septa contain thick walled dystrophic arteries with a ductular reaction but no interlobular bile ducts (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: First Aid notes FNH is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives has a liver adenoma. Its biopsy shows marked steatosis and loss of LFABP expression. Which molecular change is most likely?","choices":{"A":"CTNNB1 exon 3 activation","B":"IL6ST activation","C":"HNF1A inactivation","D":"GLI1 gene fusion","E":"DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HNF1A inactivated adenomas show loss of LFABP expression, marked steatosis, and minimal malignant risk. CTNNB1 mutations define beta catenin adenomas, gp130 activation defines inflammatory adenomas, GLI1 fusions define sonic hedgehog adenomas, and DNAJB1 PRKACA drives fibrolamellar HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory adenomas overexpress CRP and SAA and are associated with obesity (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: adenomas are sheets of normal appearing hepatocytes with arterial supply but no portal tracts or bile ducts (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CTNNB1 mutations define beta c"}},{"id":"sdl17_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman who takes oral contraceptives has sudden right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. CT shows hemoperitoneum from a bleeding liver mass. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","C":"Simple hepatic cyst","D":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","E":"Amebic liver abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is linked to oral contraceptives and is prone to spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage or rupture, a surgical emergency. FNH carries no rupture risk, fibrolamellar HCC is malignant without this exposure, a simple cyst does not bleed in this way, and an abscess would bring fever. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatocellular adenoma carries a raised risk of malignant transformation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: transformation to HCC is most likely with the beta catenin subtype or a size of 5 cm or more (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"FNH carries no rupture risk, f"}},{"id":"sdl17_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B and cirrhosis has weight loss and a liver mass invading the portal vein. Biopsy shows polygonal cells in thick trabeculae with bile plugs. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","B":"Cavernous hemangioma","C":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Colorectal metastasis","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCC cells resemble hepatocytes growing in thickened trabeculae or pseudoacinar structures with bile plugs, and HCC invades the portal and hepatic veins. Cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile, FNH and hemangioma are benign, and metastases, though the most common malignant liver tumors, are rarely solitary and would not fit HBV cirrhosis with bile plugs as well. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology, lines 3478–3490).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes aflatoxin B1 forming DNA adducts that cause a TP53 G to T transversion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes metastases are the most common malignant liver tumors overall (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholangiocarcinoma does not pr"}},{"id":"sdl17_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man without liver disease has a large liver mass. His serum tumor markers are unremarkable, and biopsy shows large oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen bundles. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","C":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","D":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","E":"Hepatic angiosarcoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibrolamellar HCC arises in young adults without cirrhosis, is AFP negative, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. FNH has a central stellate scar rather than lamellar collagen around oncocytic cells, adenoma lacks stroma, cholangiocarcinoma forms glands in desmoplastic stroma, and angiosarcoma is vascular. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes contrast CT or MRI confirms HCC after ultrasound screening (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes HCC on ultrasound as a heterogeneous, hypervascular lesion with irregular borders (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"FNH has a central stellate sca"}},{"id":"sdl17_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old farmer from rural West Africa without cirrhosis develops HCC. He ate mold contaminated stored peanuts for decades. Which molecular change most likely links this exposure to his tumor?","choices":{"A":"KRAS activating mutation","B":"APC truncating mutation","C":"BRAF V600E mutation","D":"TP53 point mutation","E":"MYCN amplification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dietary mycotoxin from Aspergillus on stored crops forms DNA adducts that cause a TP53 inactivating G to T transversion, lowering p53 function. Robbins notes aflatoxin acts synergistically with HBV. KRAS, APC, BRAF, and MYCN changes are not the described route. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists HBV with or without cirrhosis, and all other causes of cirrhosis, as HCC associations (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: HCC risk factors include cirrhosis from hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary sclerosing cholangitis and ulcerative colitis has a firm gray white sclerotic liver mass. Which finding best separates it from HCC?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive glands","B":"Bile plugs in tumor","C":"AFP positive cells","D":"HepPar1 positive cells","E":"CD31 positive channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, linked to PSC, forms malignant glands in desmoplastic stroma that are mucin positive and do not make bile. Bile plugs, AFP, and HepPar1 favor HCC, and CD31 marks angiosarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3510–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who worked for decades at a PVC plastics plant has an ill defined hemorrhagic red purple liver mass. Which immunostains are most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Glypican 3 and AFP","B":"CK7 and CK19","C":"CD31 and ERG","D":"Chromogranin and CD56","E":"CD117 and DOG1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vinyl chloride exposure and a hemorrhagic red purple mass point to angiosarcoma, which is positive for the endothelial markers CD31, CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen. Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK7 and CK19 fit cholangiocarcinoma, and the others fit neuroendocrine and stromal tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3531).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links hepatic angiosarcoma to arsenic and vinyl chloride (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists thorium dioxide exposure for angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK","E":"Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK"}},{"id":"sdl17_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C has a liver mass. Biopsy shows polygonal tumor cells in trabeculae with bile production. Which stain is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive","B":"ERG positive","C":"Chromogranin positive","D":"HepPar1 positive","E":"Desmin positive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HCC is positive for AFP, HepPar1, and glypican 3 and negative for mucin. Mucin fits cholangiocarcinoma, ERG fits angiosarcoma, and chromogranin and desmin fit neuroendocrine and muscle tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, HCC diagnostic markers, lines 3502–3509).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cellular atypia, pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, and a trabecular pattern in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: the lecture ties HCC to chronic HBV, HCV, cirrhosis, alcohol, MASLD, and aflatoxin (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had slowly developing hepatic vein obstruction from a hypercoagulable state for several years. Her liver biopsy is reviewed for chronic changes. Which finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pericentral fibrosis","B":"Periportal fibrin deposits","C":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","D":"Bridging portal fibrosis","E":"Diffuse microvesicular fat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic or slowly developing Budd Chiari syndrome, pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis develops and the major hepatic veins show organizing thrombi. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, periductal fibrosis fits PSC, bridging portal fibrosis links portal tracts in chronic hepatitis rather than centering on veins, and microvesicular fat fits AFLP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists portal vein and splenic vein thrombosis as prehepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid notes portal vein thrombosis causes no hepatomegaly unless liver disease preexists (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 34 weeks of gestation with preeclampsia has rising transaminases, and her INR climbs to 2.1 (reference 0.8–1.2). Which step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Plasmapheresis","B":"Liver transplant","C":"Oral corticosteroids","D":"Prompt delivery","E":"Heparin infusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclamptic liver injury, severe coagulopathy indicates advanced disease requiring prompt delivery or termination of pregnancy. Plasmapheresis, steroids, and transplant do not remove the placental cause, and heparin would worsen bleeding with an INR of 2.1. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia clinical presentation, lines 3418–3420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in eclampsia, intrahepatic hematomas can dissect beneath Glisson capsule (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: periportal hemorrhage into the space of Disse leads to periportal coagulative necrosis in preeclampsia (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Plasmapheresis, steroids, and "}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has an incidental liver nodule with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. What underlies the formation of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Clonal CTNNB1 mutation","C":"Chronic HBV infection","D":"Dietary aflatoxin exposure","E":"Aberrant local arteries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Focal nodular hyperplasia is a nonneoplastic hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to local vascular malformations or altered arterial flow. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic inflow obstruction, CTNNB1 mutations fit adenoma, and HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes FNH as a hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to an aberrant dystrophic artery (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes radiating fibrous bands from the central scar containing abnormal vessels (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a liver mass that enhances brightly in the early contrast phase and washes out in the delayed phase on triphasic CT. What explains this enhancement pattern?","choices":{"A":"Portal venous supply","B":"Venous outflow block","C":"Arterial tumor supply","D":"Intratumoral fat content","E":"Central fibrous scar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arterial phase hyperenhancement with venous or delayed washout is the hallmark of HCC and reflects a predominant arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. The tumor is fed by arteries rather than the portal vein, so contrast arrives early and leaves early, and a central scar fits FNH. Intratumoral fat does not explain early arterial enhancement with washout. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, characteristic image finding, lines 3491–3494).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes multiphase imaging with early arterial hyperenhancement and rapid washout in portal and delayed phases (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be a unifocal mass, multifocal nodules, or a diffusely infiltrative tumor (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with recurrent intrahepatic duct stones has weight loss and dull right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a solitary firm mass in the left hepatic lobe with dilated ducts upstream of it. Needle biopsy shows malignant glands embedded in abundant desmoplastic stroma. Which additional histologic finding is most likely in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Bile production by tumor cells","B":"Prominent perineural invasion","C":"Tumor cells dissecting sinusoids","D":"Polygonal cells in trabeculae","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatolithiasis is a risk factor for intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, a firm, gray-white, sclerotic adenocarcinoma of biliary epithelium with malignant glands in abundant desmoplastic stroma that shows prominent perineural and lymphovascular invasion. Bile production by tumor cells is a feature of hepatocellular carcinoma; cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile. Malignant endothelial cells dissecting along sinusoids are the pattern of hepatic angiosarcoma. Polygonal cells in trabeculae are the growth pattern of hepatocellular carcinoma. Ovarian-type stroma defines mucinous cystic neoplasms (mostly pancreatic, rarely hepatobiliary), which are cystic lesions rather than an infiltrating desmoplastic adenocarcinoma; Robbins lists hepatobiliary mucinous cystic neoplasms only as a less common precursor of cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, HCC, and angiosarcoma morphology, lines 3501–3528); First Aid 2025 GI (cholangiocarcinoma histology); Robbins Ch. 18 (mucinous cystic neoplasms as less common precursors, lines 4511–4512).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes cholangiocarcinoma histology as infiltrating neoplastic glands with desmoplastic stroma (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid notes that cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man reviews his medical records with his physician, which list several past liver diagnoses. Which of these places him at risk for hepatocellular carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hemochromatosis","B":"Gilbert syndrome","C":"Rotor syndrome","D":"Cavernous hemangioma","E":"Focal nodular hyperplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HCC risk factors include chronic HBV and HCV, cirrhosis of any cause including hereditary hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency, and aflatoxin. Gilbert and Rotor syndromes are benign, FNH has no malignant potential, and First Aid describes hemangioma as a benign venous malformation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links HCC to Wilson disease and autoimmune disease among the causes of cirrhosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp calls HCC the most common primary liver malignancy in adults (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — inherited metabolic liver diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Gilbert and Rotor syndromes ar"}}],"18":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which genetic variants explain a cholesterol gallstone prevalence above 75 percent in Pima, Hopi, and Navajo groups?","choices":{"A":"ABCG8 variants","B":"HFE variants","C":"PKD1 variants","D":"CFTR variants","E":"ATP7B variants"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Native American groups such as the Pima, Hopi, and Navajo exceed 75 percent prevalence of cholesterol gallstones because of ABCG8 transporter variants. HFE and ATP7B variants cause hemochromatosis and Wilson disease (lecture SDL 16), CFTR variants cause cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 19), and PKD1 variants cause polycystic kidney disease (Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt lines 4457–4462). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, ethnicity and genetics, lines 3544–3547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the 8 Fs of gallstones, including fair, feeds (TPN), fasting, and fibrates (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are the majority of gallstones and are generally radiolucent (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is cholesterolosis, with foam cells filling the lamina propria, also called?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Strawberry gallbladder","C":"Hydrops of gallbladder","D":"Courvoisier gallbladder","E":"Phrygian cap gallbladder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are associated with cholesterolosis, or strawberry gallbladder, in which the lamina propria contains abundant cholesterol laden foam cells. Porcelain gallbladder is wall calcification, and hydrops is a dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstone morphology, lines 3551–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure cholesterol stones are pale yellow with a radiating crystalline palisade on cross section (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: fibrate therapy and multiparity are conditions associated with cholesterol stones (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do brown pigment gallstones form?","choices":{"A":"Sterile gallbladder bile","B":"Main pancreatic duct lumen","C":"Hepatocyte bile canaliculi","D":"Gallbladder mucosal crypts","E":"Infected large bile ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and are soft, laminated, and greasy. Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile. The main pancreatic duct, hepatocyte canaliculi, and mucosal crypts are not where they form, because brown stones need infection of the large bile ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, black versus brown stones, lines 3567–3577).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links brown pigment stones to Clonorchis sinensis and E coli infection of the biliary system (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: First Aid lists black pigment stones as radiopaque and brown pigment stones as radiolucent (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa","C":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa","D":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa"}},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what keeps cholesterol in colloidal solution in bile?","choices":{"A":"Albumin and globulins","B":"Mucin and calcium","C":"Bilirubin and mucin","D":"Bile salts and lecithin","E":"Bicarbonate and chloride"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Water insoluble cholesterol must be incorporated into mixed micelles with bile salts and phospholipids such as lecithin to stay in colloidal solution. Mucin and calcium instead help stones grow. Albumin and globulins and the electrolytes bicarbonate and chloride cannot form micelles; that takes amphipathic bile salts with lecithin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, solubilization requirement, lines 3583–3585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists high biliary cholesterol, low bile acids or lecithin, and biliary stasis as general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: supersaturation occurs when hepatocytes hypersecrete cholesterol or bile salt and lecithin secretion drops (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albumin and globulins and the ","C":"Mucin and calcium instead help","E":"Albumin and globulins and the "}},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how much of a cholesterol gallstone is crystalline cholesterol monohydrate?","choices":{"A":"Less than 10 percent","B":"Close to 40 percent","C":"Close to 20 percent","D":"Close to 30 percent","E":"More than 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate, with variable calcium salts and a mucin glycoprotein scaffold. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, composition, lines 3596–3597).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states cholesterol stones make up about 80 percent of stones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes cholesterol stones as mixed, with more cholesterol than calcium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 1 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although most bilirubin in bile is conjugated, about 1 percent undergoes spontaneous or enzymatic deconjugation. Chronic hypersecretion of conjugated bilirubin therefore yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, deconjugation, lines 3604–3607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: unconjugated bilirubin binds free calcium in sterile bile to form insoluble calcium bilirubinate (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp states black pigment stones consist mainly of calcium bilirubinate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of acute cholecystitis cases are calculous?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 60 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases and follows obstruction of the gallbladder neck or cystic duct by an impacted stone. Acalculous disease accounts for about 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3618–3621).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls cholecystitis the most common complication of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists IV antibiotics and laparoscopic cholecystectomy for acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which toxic product do mucosal phospholipases convert luminal lecithin in acute calculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Sphingomyelin","B":"Lysolecithin","C":"Phosphatidylserine","D":"Cholesteryl ester","E":"Ceramide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mucosal phospholipases hydrolyze luminal lecithin into toxic lysolecithin, which breaks down the protective mucus and exposes the epithelium to bile salt detergent action. Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylserine, cholesteryl esters, and ceramide are not what phospholipase hydrolysis of lecithin produces. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3621–3623).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distention compresses mucosal blood flow and causes ischemia with prostaglandin mediated inflammation (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: E coli and Enterococcus may superinfect the inflamed gallbladder (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylser"}},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Impacted gallstone","B":"Clonorchis infestation","C":"Cystic artery ischemia","D":"Sphincter of Oddi spasm","E":"Biliary atresia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis is driven mainly by ischemia of the cystic artery, an end artery without collaterals, compounded by stasis, viscous bile, and sludge. An impacted stone defines calculous disease. Clonorchis is a risk for pigment stones and cholangiocarcinoma, sphincter of Oddi spasm is not a listed driver, and biliary atresia is an infant duct disease rather than a complication of critical illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3637–3642).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes acalculous cholecystitis is seen in critically ill patients and can follow CMV infection (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp attributes acalculous cholecystitis to reduced perfusion, biliary stasis, and infection (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy for long-standing gallstones. The resected wall shows dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa, with very little epithelium left. Which variant is this?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","C":"Hydrops of the gallbladder","D":"Cholesterolosis of the mucosa","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis is a variant of chronic cholecystitis with dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa and minimal remaining epithelium; it carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic calcification of the wall. Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis is a shrunken, nodular wall full of foamy macrophages from ruptured Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Hydrops is a dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Cholesterolosis is lipid-laden macrophages in the lamina propria, the strawberry gallbladder. Sources: course SDL 18 reading (Robbins, Liver and gallbladder, chronic cholecystitis variants); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, morphology and variants, p103).","boardPrep":"Step 1: porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, and long-standing gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gallstones are present in more than 90 percent of chronic cholecystitis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — risk factors for gallbladder carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what defines porcelain gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic calcification","B":"Foamy macrophage infiltrate","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Clear mucoid distention","E":"Transmural wall necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Porcelain gallbladder is a rare form of chronic cholecystitis with extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall. Foamy macrophages fit xanthogranulomatous disease, hyaline fibrosis fits hyalinizing cholecystitis, and mucoid distention is hydrops. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, porcelain gallbladder, lines 3667–3668).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes porcelain gallbladder is usually found incidentally on imaging (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder). COMLEX: First Aid generally recommends prophylactic cholecystectomy because of cancer risk (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does gallstone ileus usually obstruct the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Ligament of Treitz","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Ileocecal valve","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A large stone eroding through a cholecystenteric fistula can cause gallstone ileus, with obstruction at the ileocecal valve. First Aid adds that the ileocecal valve is the narrowest point, so the stone lodges there rather than at the ligament of Treitz or in the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3684–3686); First Aid 2025 GI (gallstone ileus: ileocecal valve, narrowest point).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Rigler triad of pneumobilia, small bowel obstruction, and an ectopic gallstone (First Aid 2025 GI gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists large gallstones, acute cholecystitis, and malignancy as fistula risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","B":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","C":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","E":"First Aid adds that the ileoce"}},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of gallbladder carcinoma cases are gallstones present?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and are the single most important risk factor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, risk factors, lines 3691–3693).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which lesion is a precursor of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyp","B":"Flat dysplasia","C":"Adenomyomatosis","D":"Cholesterol polyp","E":"Rokitansky sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma progresses through flat dysplasia or in situ lesions, intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms, and intestinal metaplasia. Hyperplastic and cholesterol polyps, adenomyomatosis, and Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are not on the lecture's precursor list; Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are the mucosal outpouchings of chronic cholecystitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, precursor lesions, lines 3704–3705).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mucosal irritation drives persistent epithelial injury and regeneration in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, occur in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the 5 year survival of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Under 10 percent","B":"Above 30 percent","C":"Above 50 percent","D":"Above 70 percent","E":"Above 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma has an extremely poor prognosis, with most tumors found at an advanced, unresectable stage and a 5 year survival under 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, prognosis, lines 3721–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in under 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: the tumor appears as an exophytic papillary mass or diffuse firm thickening of the fundic wall (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with obesity, three prior pregnancies, and oral contraceptive use has gallstones on ultrasound that do not show on a plain radiograph. What are her stones most likely made of?","choices":{"A":"Calcium bilirubinate polymer","B":"Calcium carbonate salts","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals","D":"Cholesterol monohydrate","E":"Uric acid crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Female sex hormones, multiparity, oral contraceptives, and obesity raise cholesterol secretion into bile, and cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, with only 10 to 20 percent opaque. Calcium bilirubinate stones are black pigment stones, about half or more of which are radiopaque. Uric acid is not among the gallstone types the lecture describes (cholesterol, black pigment, brown pigment). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstones, lines 3535–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: estrogen raises hepatic LDL receptor expression and HMG CoA reductase activity (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol saturation increases progressively with age (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis has small, friable, dark, spiculated stones in his gallbladder. Which stone type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Brown pigment stones","B":"Black pigment stones","C":"Cholesterol stones","D":"Calcium carbonate stones","E":"Calcium oxalate stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hemolytic anemias such as hereditary spherocytosis and sickle cell anemia cause black pigment stones, which are small, multiple, friable, and dark with molded or spiculated contours. Brown stones form in infected ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, pigment gallstones, lines 3558–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mechanical prosthetic heart valves are also associated with pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic hemolysis and liver disease as risk factors for black pigment stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from rural Southeast Asia with Clonorchis infection has soft, laminated, greasy stones in his large bile ducts. What are these stones mainly composed of?","choices":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin gel","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid","D":"Cystine and xanthine crystals","E":"Calcium soaps and bilirubinate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large ducts and are made of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin, cholesterol, and calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate. They are characteristically radiolucent. Cystine and xanthine are not gallstone components; First Aid places cystine stones in the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, brown pigment stones, lines 3574–3577); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (cystinuria: cystine stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states brown pigment stones usually form outside the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: pigment stones are more common in rural populations and non Western countries (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with sickle cell anemia forms black gallstones. Her hepatic bilirubin conjugation is normal. Why does free unconjugated bilirubin rise in her bile?","choices":{"A":"Absent hepatic conjugation","B":"Bacterial infection of the bile","C":"Lower biliary calcium levels","D":"Deconjugation of excess output","E":"Cholesterol supersaturated bile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic hemolysis the liver secretes excessive conjugated bilirubin into bile. About 1 percent of it deconjugates, so chronic hypersecretion yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin that binds calcium. Infection drives brown stones, and supersaturation drives cholesterol stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, pathogenesis of black pigment stones, lines 3598–3610).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links pigment stones to Crohn disease, chronic hemolysis, alcoholic cirrhosis, and TPN (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: black stones contain calcium bilirubinate, carbonate, phosphate, mucin, and little cholesterol (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman loses 30 kg in the 6 months after bariatric surgery and develops gallstones. Which change most directly lets cholesterol crystals accumulate into stones?","choices":{"A":"Biliary bacterial infection","B":"Excess bilirubin secretion","C":"Gallbladder hypomotility","D":"Low cholesterol secretion","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid weight loss and gallbladder stasis are major risk factors. Supersaturated cholesterol precipitates as monohydrate crystals, and gallbladder hypomotility or stasis lets the crystals accumulate. More lecithin and less cholesterol would protect against stones. Biliary infection gives brown pigment stones in the ducts, and excess bilirubin secretion, as in hemolysis, gives black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1 and 2, risk factors and nucleation and stasis, lines 3547, 3589–3592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists fasting or rapid weight loss among the 8 Fs of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists biliary stasis among the general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"More lecithin and less cholest"}},{"id":"sdl18_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with obesity has cholesterol supersaturated bile containing crystals. What lets the nucleated crystals grow into macroscopic stones?","choices":{"A":"Mucin glycoprotein gel","B":"Calcium phosphate shell","C":"Fibrin clot matrix","D":"Collagen fiber scaffold","E":"Bacterial biofilm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Epithelial hypersecretion of mucin glycoproteins forms a gel matrix that traps nucleated crystals and allows progressive accretion into macroscopic stones. A calcium phosphate shell, fibrin clot, collagen scaffold, or bacterial biofilm is not the matrix; cholesterol stones have a mucin glycoprotein scaffold, and calcium phosphate belongs to black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, mucus secretion and accretion, lines 3593–3595).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol is water insoluble and needs mixed micelles with bile salts and lecithin (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists malabsorption syndromes among the classic risk factors for cholesterol stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A calcium phosphate shell, fib"}},{"id":"sdl18_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain, fever, and inspiratory arrest on palpation. Ultrasound shows a stone in the gallbladder neck. Which histologic change is most likely early on?","choices":{"A":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","B":"Edema and neutrophils","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Foamy xanthoma cells","E":"Dystrophic wall calcium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early acute calculous cholecystitis shows edema, congestion, mucosal erosions, and early neutrophilic infiltration. Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hyaline fibrosis, xanthoma cells, and calcification are features of chronic disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3 and 4, acute calculous morphology and chronic variants, lines 3629–3636, 3664–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Murphy sign as inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation, with pain that may radiate to the right shoulder (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists gallbladder wall thickening, edema, and pericholecystic fluid on ultrasound (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hy"}},{"id":"sdl18_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man in the ICU after severe burns develops fever and right upper quadrant tenderness. Ultrasound shows a distended gallbladder with sludge and no stones. Which outcome is more frequent in his condition than in the stone related form?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain wall calcification","B":"Common duct stone impaction","C":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma","D":"Cholecystenteric fistula ileus","E":"Gangrene and perforation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis in critically ill patients is recognized late, so gangrene and perforation occur at a significantly higher frequency than in calculous disease. Porcelain calcification and adenocarcinoma follow long standing chronic cholecystitis, and common duct impaction and fistula ileus need stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3643–3650).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acalculous disease include sepsis with hypotension, trauma, burns, cardiac surgery, diabetes, and vasculitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised CRP and leukocytosis in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes has acute gallbladder inflammation, and CT shows gas within the gallbladder wall. Which form of the disease is this?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous cholecystitis","B":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","C":"Gallbladder empyema","D":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe acute cholecystitis can show gas forming bacterial infection, called emphysematous cholecystitis. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, empyema is a gallbladder full of pus, and xanthogranulomatous and hyalinizing forms are chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3636).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes cholecystitis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours with localized guarding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp states a HIDA scan is positive when the gallbladder is not seen on delayed or morphine augmented imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has a porcelain gallbladder on imaging. Which malignancy of that organ is she at greatest risk of?","choices":{"A":"Angiosarcoma","B":"MALT lymphoma","C":"Carcinoid tumor","D":"Hepatoblastoma","E":"Adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic cholecystitis carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma, particularly with porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, or long standing stones. The lecture names adenocarcinoma as the usual gallbladder cancer; angiosarcoma, MALT lymphoma, and carcinoid tumor are not linked to porcelain gallbladder, and hepatoblastoma is a rare childhood liver tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3677–3680); Robbins Ch. 18 (hepatoblastoma occurs in children).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces chronic cholecystitis with porcelain gallbladder to gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes the cancer risk is mostly adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lecture names adenocarcino","D":"The lecture names adenocarcino"}},{"id":"sdl18_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with recurrent cholecystitis has vomiting and abdominal distention. Radiographs show air in the biliary tree, dilated small bowel, and an ectopic calcified stone in the bowel. Which mechanism explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous infection","B":"Choledochal cyst rupture","C":"Cholecystenteric fistula","D":"Duodenal ulcer perforation","E":"Mesenteric ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large stone can erode from the gallbladder into the duodenum or colon through a cholecystenteric fistula, letting air and bacteria into the biliary tree and causing gallstone ileus. Emphysematous infection is a gas forming infection of the gallbladder itself, not a stone in the bowel. Choledochal cyst rupture, duodenal ulcer perforation, and mesenteric ischemia would not put air in the biliary tree with a calcified stone in the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, biliary enteric fistula, lines 3684–3686).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallstone ileus raises the risk of acute cholangitis and often needs surgery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes episodic small bowel obstruction with intermittent diffuse pain in gallstone ileus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic cholecystitis has a shrunken, nodular, markedly thickened gallbladder wall filled with lipid laden foamy macrophages. What process most likely produced these cells?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic wall calcification","B":"Cystic artery thrombosis","C":"Clostridial gas infection","D":"Ruptured Rokitansky sinuses","E":"Hemolytic bile pigment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis forms when ruptured Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses spill bile into the wall, drawing abundant foamy, lipid laden xanthoma cells. Calcification makes porcelain gallbladder, and gas infection is emphysematous disease. Cystic artery ischemia is the driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis, not of xanthoma cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, lines 3671–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyalinizing cholecystitis is dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall with little epithelium (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and a firm thickened fundic wall. She then becomes jaundiced. What does her jaundice most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"Early localized disease","B":"Porta hepatis invasion","C":"Benign stone passage","D":"Paraneoplastic hemolysis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gallbladder carcinoma, jaundice indicates direct invasion or metastasis into the porta hepatis or common bile duct, an advanced stage. Jaundice therefore does not mean early localized disease, and benign stone passage, paraneoplastic hemolysis, and drug cholestasis do not fit a firm, infiltrating fundic mass with weight loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, jaundice and advanced stage, lines 3716–3717).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder carcinoma has a female to male ratio above 2 to 1 and high incidence in Native American, Hispanic, Chilean, Bolivian, and Northern Indian groups (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PSC and Clonorchis infection as gallbladder adenocarcinoma risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a resected gallbladder carcinoma with marked aneuploidy. Which tumor suppressor change is found in about half of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"TP53 loss of function","B":"APC loss of function","C":"VHL loss of function","D":"SMAD4 loss of function","E":"BRCA1 loss of function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of function TP53 mutations occur in about 50 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and cause marked aneuploidy, alongside gain of function changes in EGF receptor family members and RAS signaling. APC, VHL, SMAD4, and BRCA1 are not among the drivers the lecture lists for this tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic S typhi carriage and parasitic infection are chronic inflammatory risk factors (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms are precursor lesions (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with symptoms like chronic cholecystitis is found to have gallbladder carcinoma with direct liver invasion. What most explains the poor outlook of this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Chemotherapy induced toxicity","B":"Frequent bone metastasis","C":"Advanced stage at diagnosis","D":"Hormone driven growth","E":"Early brain metastasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Symptoms mimic gallstones or chronic cholecystitis, so most tumors present at an advanced, unresectable stage with liver invasion, peritoneal spread, or nodal metastasis. Chemotherapy toxicity and hormone driven growth are not the reason, and the spread is to liver, peritoneum, and nodes rather than bone or brain; fewer than 20% are diagnosed before surgery, and 5 year survival is under 10%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, clinical presentation and prognosis, lines 3711–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma is initially asymptomatic before an RUQ mass and pain appear (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and weight loss are the presenting symptoms (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has cholesterol gallstones on ultrasound, but her plain abdominal radiograph looks normal. About what share of cholesterol stones contain enough calcium to be radiopaque?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 percent","B":"50 to 75 percent","C":"90 to 95 percent","D":"1 to 2 percent","E":"30 to 40 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, and only 10 to 20 percent contain enough calcium carbonate to be radiopaque. About 50 to 75 percent of black pigment stones are radiopaque. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol and black stone morphology, lines 3551–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists Crohn disease, advanced age, estrogen therapy, and rapid weight loss among cholesterol stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists progesterone excess and malabsorption among classic cholesterol stone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease had his terminal ileum resected and now has black gallstones. How does his ileal disease promote these stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess cholesterol absorption","B":"Biliary bacterial growth","C":"Reduced bilirubin output","D":"Lost bile salt reabsorption","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ileal disease, impaired bile salt reabsorption leads the liver to secrete excess conjugated bilirubin into bile, and the deconjugated fraction precipitates with calcium as black stones. Bacterial growth drives brown stones. More lecithin would help keep cholesterol in solution and plays no part in black stones, which come from excess bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, hemolysis and ileal disease, lines 3601–3603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal resection or bypass is an associated condition of pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: First Aid lists Crohn disease among pigment stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with acute calculous cholecystitis develops spiking fevers from bacterial superinfection of her gallbladder. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pyogenes","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Candida albicans","E":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bacterial superinfection of acute calculous cholecystitis is usually from enteric organisms such as E coli and Enterococcus. Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus aureus, Candida, and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are not the enteric bacteria the lecture names. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3624–3626).","boardPrep":"Step 1: if the gallbladder is filled with pure pus, the condition is termed empyema (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists fever, malaise, and fatigue in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman has a palpable, nontender, distended gallbladder from a long standing obstruction. At surgery it is filled with clear mucoid fluid. Which condition is this?","choices":{"A":"Hydrops of gallbladder","B":"Gallbladder empyema","C":"Porcelain gallbladder","D":"Strawberry gallbladder","E":"Emphysematous gallbladder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Empyema is filled with pus, porcelain gallbladder is calcified, strawberry gallbladder is cholesterolosis, and emphysematous disease has gas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, hydrops of the gallbladder, lines 3675–3676).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cholecystitis can be complicated by pericholecystic abscess or rupture with peritonitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: chronic cholecystitis can lead to ascending cholangitis and sepsis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman from Bolivia has a gallbladder carcinoma resected after 20 years of gallstones. Her oncologist orders tumor profiling to look for a targetable driver. Which alteration is most likely to be found?","choices":{"A":"APC tumor suppressor loss","B":"VHL tumor suppressor loss","C":"EGFR family activation","D":"BCR ABL kinase fusion","E":"JAK2 V617F activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lecture lists gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, and in downstream RAS signaling, alongside TP53 loss, as the molecular drivers of gallbladder carcinoma. APC loss is the classic colorectal driver, VHL loss drives clear cell renal carcinoma, BCR ABL defines CML, and JAK2 V617F drives myeloproliferative neoplasms; none is among the drivers the lecture lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in less than 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma arises from glandular tissue of the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with acute cholecystitis worsens, and at surgery his gallbladder lumen is filled entirely with pus. What is this complication called?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder hydrops","B":"Porcelain gallbladder","C":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","D":"Biliary sludge","E":"Gallbladder empyema"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When the lumen of an acutely inflamed gallbladder fills with pure pus, it is termed gallbladder empyema. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, hydrops holds clear mucoid fluid, and porcelain gallbladder is calcified. Biliary sludge is viscous bile with microcrystals, a feature of acalculous disease, not pus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3632).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the inflamed gallbladder is tense and red to green black from subserosal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes a sonographic Murphy sign in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the incidence of pancreas divisum, the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 percent","B":"40 to 50 percent","C":"3 to 10 percent","D":"0.1 to 0.5 percent","E":"60 to 70 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with an incidence of 3 to 10 percent, and about 95 percent of affected people have no symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3738).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks in pancreas divisum (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a raised risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain in pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas divisum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, through which route does the bulk of the gland drain in pancreas divisum?","choices":{"A":"Major papilla via Wirsung","B":"Ampulla via common bile duct","C":"Minor papilla via Wirsung","D":"Major papilla via Santorini","E":"Minor papilla via Santorini"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pancreas divisum the dorsal and ventral duct systems fail to fuse, so the bulk of the gland, from the dorsal primordium, drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini. The duct of Wirsung drains only part of the head through the major papilla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3734).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 5 percent of people with pancreas divisum develop recurrent acute or chronic pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the fetal duct systems normally fuse between the 5th and 8th gestational weeks (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of postmortem examinations is ectopic pancreatic tissue found?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas, aberrantly situated pancreatic tissue derived from gut endoderm, is present in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations, usually as an incidental subepithelial rest. About 15, 30, and 50 percent overstate how often it is found, and 0.1 percent understates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3758); Robbins Ch. 19 (ectopic pancreas in about 2 percent of careful routine postmortem examinations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins notes ectopic rests may cause pain from localized inflammation or incite mucosal bleeding (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreatic nests are usually incidental subepithelial findings (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which event initiates acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cell destruction","B":"Stellate cell collagen deposition","C":"IgG4 plasma cell infiltration","D":"Trypsin activation in gland","E":"Biliary epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute pancreatitis begins with inappropriate intrapancreatic activation of trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates other zymogens and drives autodigestion and fat necrosis. Stellate cell collagen deposition drives chronic pancreatitis. Islet loss is a late chronic change, IgG4 plasma cells mark autoimmune type 1 chronic pancreatitis, and biliary epithelial apoptosis is not the initiating event. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3778–3782).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists didanosine, stavudine, furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide, glucocorticoids, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid gives the I GET SMASHED mnemonic for causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum marker is the most sensitive and specific for acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Serum lipase","B":"Serum amylase","C":"Serum ALT","D":"Serum calcium","E":"Serum CRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Serum lipase is the most sensitive and specific marker. It rises within 4 to 12 hours and stays raised for 8 to 14 days, whereas amylase rises quickly but returns to baseline within 3 to 5 days. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3804–3808).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid diagnoses acute pancreatitis by 2 of 3 criteria, including enzymes at 3 times the upper limit of normal (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ESR, CRP, and LDH in pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells deposit collagen in chronic pancreatitis after activation by TGF beta and PDGF?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells","B":"Centroacinar duct cells","C":"Pancreatic stellate cells","D":"Resident tissue mast cells","E":"Ductal epithelial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repeated acinar injury stimulates local TGF beta and PDGF, which activate pancreatic stellate cells, the periacinar myofibroblasts, to deposit collagen and cause progressive fibrosis and acinar loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3782–3785).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists dystrophic calcifications and parenchymal atrophy in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low fecal elastase 1 in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what makes up the wall of a pancreatic pseudocyst?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous granulation tissue","B":"Glycogen rich cuboidal cells","C":"Tall mucinous columnar cells","D":"Dense ovarian type stroma","E":"Papillary ductal epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pseudocyst lacks a true epithelial lining. Its wall is fibrous tissue, granulation tissue, and necrotic debris with saponified fat. Glycogen rich cuboidal cells line serous cystadenomas, and mucinous epithelium with ovarian stroma lines MCNs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3826–3829).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudocysts account for about 75 percent of all pancreatic cysts (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a pseudocyst as a fibrous capsule surrounding pancreatic fluid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are tied to serous cystic neoplasms?","choices":{"A":"KRAS on 12p","B":"VHL on 3p","C":"GNAS on 20q","D":"RNF43 on 17q","E":"SMAD4 on 18q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas are strongly associated with loss of function of the VHL tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. KRAS and GNAS mark mucinous lesions, and SMAD4 marks invasive progression. RNF43 loss marks mucinous cystic neoplasms and IPMNs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3841–3845).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous cystadenomas occur twice as often in females, peak in the 6th to 7th decades, and favor the tail (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: serous cystadenomas carry virtually no risk of malignant transformation (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature is pathognomonic of a mucinous cystic neoplasm?","choices":{"A":"Glycogen rich lining","B":"Main duct papillae","C":"Granulation tissue wall","D":"Honeycomb microcysts","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MCNs are lined by tall mucin producing columnar epithelium backed by a pathognomonic dense ovarian type stroma that expresses estrogen and progesterone receptors and inhibin. Main duct papillae fit IPMN, and honeycomb microcysts with glycogen rich cells fit serous cystadenoma. A wall of granulation and fibrous tissue without epithelium is a pseudocyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3853).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCNs are driven by KRAS mutations in about 50 percent and RNF43 loss in about 33 percent (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: up to one third of MCNs harbor an invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 5 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 60 percent of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head, about 15 percent in the body, and about 5 percent in the tail. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3878–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic adenocarcinoma is often metastatic at presentation, with average survival of about 1 year (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid names tobacco smoking the strongest risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which alteration is the earliest driver in low grade PanIN 1 lesions?","choices":{"A":"TP53 inactivation","B":"SMAD4 inactivation","C":"CDKN2A inactivation","D":"KRAS activation","E":"BRCA2 inactivation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activating KRAS point mutations on 12p occur in more than 90 percent of cases and are the earliest driver in PanIN 1. CDKN2A loss appears at PanIN 2, TP53 and SMAD4 loss at PanIN 3, and BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CDKN2A is inactivated in about 30 percent by mutation and up to 90 percent with promoter hypermethylation (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid names a KRAS activating mutation as the most common genomic abnormality (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what causes the Trousseau sign in pancreatic cancer?","choices":{"A":"Tumor procoagulant mucins","B":"Platelet antibody consumption","C":"Antithrombin gene mutation","D":"Factor V Leiden variant","E":"Estrogen driven clotting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Trousseau sign, migratory thrombophlebitis or recurrent venous thrombosis, is caused by tumor derived procoagulant mucins. Platelet antibodies, antithrombin or factor V Leiden variants, and estrogen are not the mechanism, because the clotting comes from the tumor itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, systemic presentations, lines 3885–3888).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes migratory thrombophlebitis as redness and tenderness on palpation of the extremities (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp links a hypercoagulable state to superficial thrombophlebitis in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which protein is the amyloid in insulinomas derived?","choices":{"A":"Proinsulin","B":"Calcitonin","C":"Light chains","D":"Transthyretin","E":"Islet amylin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Insulinomas show monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with characteristic amyloid derived from amylin. Calcitonin amyloid fits medullary thyroid carcinoma, light chains fit AL amyloid, and transthyretin fits senile amyloid. First Aid also lists islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), not proinsulin, as the islet amyloid protein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinoma morphology, lines 3911–3915); First Aid 2025 Pathology (islet amyloid polypeptide from amylin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90 percent of insulinomas are benign, solitary, encapsulated nodules under 2 cm (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp states insulinomas arise from beta cells with autonomous insulin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid also lists islet amy"}},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of gastrinomas occur with MEN1 syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1, often multifocal and duodenal, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are usually single. About 10 percent of insulinomas are MEN1 related. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3932).","boardPrep":"Step 1: more than 50 percent of gastrinomas are locally invasive or metastatic at diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes autonomous gastrin release driving parietal cell acid secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which skin rash is characteristic of a glucagonoma?","choices":{"A":"Painful erythema nodosum","B":"Violaceous livedo reticularis","C":"Velvety acanthosis nigricans","D":"Necrolytic migratory erythema","E":"Itchy dermatitis herpetiformis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucagonoma causes mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and deep vein thrombosis. The other rashes fit inflammatory bowel disease, vasculopathy, insulin resistance, and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas are most common in perimenopausal or postmenopausal women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss and thrombosis in glucagonoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other rashes fit inflammat","B":"The other rashes fit inflammat","C":"The other rashes fit inflammat","E":"The other rashes fit inflammat"}},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man who does not drink alcohol has had four episodes of acute pancreatitis since age 17. He takes no medications, ultrasound shows no gallstones, and his serum calcium and triglycerides are normal. His younger sister has also had pancreatitis, and neither parent has. MRCP shows that his dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts never joined. Genetic testing is most likely to show a variant in which gene?","choices":{"A":"ATP7B","B":"UGT1A1","C":"ABCB11","D":"CFTR","E":"HNF1A"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most people with pancreas divisum have no symptoms, and only about 5 percent develop pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the narrow minor papilla, particularly when a genetic defect that increases susceptibility to pancreatitis is also present. Recurrent pancreatitis from adolescence with an affected sibling and unaffected parents suggests a recessive susceptibility gene, and CFTR is one of the genes Robbins names in hereditary pancreatitis, along with PRSS1 and SPINK1. ATP7B mutations cause Wilson disease with hepatic and neurologic copper overload. ABCB11 encodes the canalicular bile salt export pump, and its defects cause familial intrahepatic cholestasis rather than pancreatitis. HNF1A variants cause a monogenic form of diabetes and hepatocellular adenomas, not recurrent pancreatitis. UGT1A1 variants cause Gilbert or Crigler Najjar syndrome with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum and predisposing genetic defects, lines 3726–3738); Robbins Ch. 19 (pancreas divisum; PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR in hereditary pancreatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that pancreas divisum is a common anomaly, mostly asymptomatic, that may cause chronic abdominal pain or pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists an increased risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain with pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as an etiology of pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has had 8 months of postprandial epigastric fullness and intermittent vomiting. She does not use NSAIDs, and upper endoscopy shows no ulcer or mass. CT shows a band of tissue with the same density and enhancement as the pancreatic head wrapped around the duodenum, with a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb proximal to it. Which process best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophy of pyloric muscle","B":"Peptic scarring of the pylorus","C":"Extrinsic duodenal constriction","D":"Intraluminal duodenal mucosal web","E":"Failure of gastric smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum is an annular pancreas, formed when a split ventral bud migrates around the duodenal C loop. About one third of cases are incidental, but in symptomatic patients the ring constricts the duodenum from outside and causes stenosis or obstruction, presenting in neonates or in adults with persistent vomiting and distention. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is an infant lesion at the pylorus and would not dilate the duodenal bulb. Peptic scarring of the pylorus requires ulcer disease, and endoscopy showed none. An intraluminal duodenal web is a recanalization defect inside the lumen, not an encircling band of pancreas. Gastric smooth muscle failure would leave the duodenal bulb undilated and would show no encircling tissue. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748); Robbins Ch. 19 (annular pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (annular pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that annular pancreas may cause duodenal narrowing and vomiting and is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: an annular pancreas can first cause symptoms in adulthood (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has epigastric pain and occult blood in his stool. Endoscopy shows a small submucosal gastric nodule, and biopsy shows normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastric carcinoid","B":"Gastric lipoma","C":"Gastric leiomyoma","D":"Gastric lymphoma","E":"Ectopic pancreas"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas forms subepithelial rests of normal acini, ducts, and sometimes islets, most often in the stomach and duodenum. Inflammation can cause epigastric pain, and ulceration can cause occult bleeding. A carcinoid, lipoma, leiomyoma, or lymphoma would not show normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets on biopsy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas in a Meckel diverticulum can cause peptic injury (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has epigastric pain radiating to his back, and CT shows peripancreatic fat necrosis. His serum calcium is 7.2 mg/dL (reference 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism explains his low calcium?","choices":{"A":"Fat saponification","B":"Renal calcium wasting","C":"PTH gland failure","D":"Calcitonin surge","E":"Bone mineral uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In acute pancreatitis, released lipases break down fat, and the fatty acids bind calcium to form calcium soaps, so fat saponification causes hypocalcemia. Renal wasting, parathyroid failure, a calcitonin surge, and bone uptake are not the mechanism; calcium is consumed as soaps in the necrotic fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3794–3797, 3812–3816).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes hypocalcemia in acute pancreatitis to precipitation of calcium soaps (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes acute pancreatitis pain radiates to the back and improves with leaning forward (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted with acute pancreatitis. Which admission finding counts toward her Ranson score?","choices":{"A":"AST 150 U/L","B":"Age 45 years","C":"Glucose 120 mg/dL","D":"LDH 200 U/L","E":"LDH 400 U/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ranson admission criteria include age over 55, WBC over 16,000 per microliter, glucose over 200 mg/dL, AST over 250 U/L, and LDH over 350 U/L. Only an LDH of 400 U/L meets a threshold here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, Ranson criteria concept, lines 3786–3792).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ranson criteria at 48 hours include a hematocrit fall over 10 percent and a BUN rise over 5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the 48 hour criteria include calcium under 8 mg/dL and PaO2 under 60 mm Hg (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with long standing alcohol use has steatorrhea, weight loss, and pancreatic calcifications on CT. During a painful attack his pancreatic enzymes are barely raised. Which explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rapid renal enzyme loss","B":"Enzymes bound by albumin","C":"Lab assay interference","D":"Acinar cell dropout","E":"Islet cell destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pancreatitis, amylase and lipase may be normal or only mildly raised during attacks because extensive acinar dropout leaves too little functional tissue to release enzymes. Islets are preserved until late and do not make these enzymes. The low values reflect real loss of enzyme producing tissue rather than assay interference. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3809–3811).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists fat soluble vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears below 10 percent function (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis had an acute attack 6 weeks ago and now has a walled off peripancreatic fluid collection with debris. Which complication is this lesion prone to?","choices":{"A":"Malignant transformation","B":"Islet amyloid deposits","C":"Abscess formation","D":"GNAS driven carcinoma","E":"Tuberculous granulomas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pseudocyst arises after acute pancreatitis, especially on chronic alcoholic disease, when walled off fat necrosis liquefies. Its complications are secondary infection forming an abscess, erosion into vessels or viscera, and rupture. It is not a neoplasm, so malignant change and GNAS carcinoma do not apply, islet amyloid belongs to type 2 diabetes, and tuberculous granulomas are not among its listed complications; its infectious risk is abscess. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3830–3836).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes pseudocysts raise the risk of extrinsic compression of local structures (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst). COMLEX: First Aid describes a pseudocyst as lined by granulation tissue, not epithelium (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is not a neoplasm, so malig"}},{"id":"sdl19_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a slow growing, painless, 6 cm multiloculated cyst in her pancreatic tail filled with thick, tenacious mucin. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Endoscopic drainage","B":"Percutaneous drainage","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Early resection","E":"Somatostatin analog"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A mucin filled multiloculated tail cyst in a woman is a mucinous cystic neoplasm, a precancerous lesion in which up to one third harbor invasive adenocarcinoma, so early surgical resection is mandatory. Drainage suits pseudocysts, not neoplasms. Antibiotics treat an infected pseudocyst or abscess, not a neoplasm, and a somatostatin analog does not remove a precancerous cyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3861).","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95 percent of MCNs arise in women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: TP53 and SMAD4 mutations mark the transition of MCN to invasive cancer (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a cystic lesion of the pancreatic head that arises within a dilated main duct and contains mucin producing papillae without ovarian stroma. Which mutation best distinguishes this lesion from other pancreatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"VHL inactivation","B":"GNAS activation","C":"BRAF activation","D":"HNF1A inactivation","E":"CTNNB1 activation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An IPMN arises in the larger ducts, favors the head in men, and lacks ovarian stroma. Its oncogenic GNAS mutations, in about two thirds, are unique among pancreatic cysts. VHL marks serous cystadenoma. BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are not among the cyst mutations the lecture lists (KRAS, RNF43, GNAS, VHL). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, IPMNs, lines 3862–3873).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 20 percent of IPMNs are multifocal (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs also carry KRAS mutations in about 80 percent and RNF43 mutations in about 50 percent (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are no"}},{"id":"sdl19_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has mild, progressive painless jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and a palpable nontender gallbladder. Where is his tumor most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic body","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Gallbladder fundus","E":"Proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tumors of the pancreatic head obstruct the distal common bile duct and cause obstructive jaundice with the Courvoisier sign, a palpable nontender gallbladder. Body and tail tumors present late with pain and weight loss. A jejunal mass does not obstruct the distal common bile duct, and a gallbladder fundus cancer forms a firm, thickened wall rather than a distended, nontender gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3877–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes a double duct sign on imaging in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists endoscopic ultrasound with tissue sampling for diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A jejunal mass does not obstru"}},{"id":"sdl19_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman developed diabetes a year ago and now has back pain, weight loss, and recurrent superficial vein clots. CT shows a mass in her pancreatic body. Which serum marker is most useful to track her response to treatment?","choices":{"A":"Cancer antigen 125 level","B":"Carbohydrate antigen 19 9","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Neuron specific enolase","E":"Serum alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CA 19 9 and CEA are often raised in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. They lack the sensitivity and specificity for screening but are useful to track response to treatment and detect recurrence. CA 125, PSA, neuron specific enolase, and alpha fetoprotein, an HCC marker, are not the pancreatic adenocarcinoma markers the lecture names. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3889–3893).","boardPrep":"Step 1: new onset diabetes appears in up to 50 percent of patients up to 2 years before diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists metastasis to the liver as a complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CA 125, PSA, neuron specific e","C":"CA 125, PSA, neuron specific e"}},{"id":"sdl19_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, and several relatives have had melanoma and atypical moles. Which germline gene is most likely mutated in his family?","choices":{"A":"BRCA2 gene","B":"PALB2 gene","C":"PRSS1 gene","D":"SERPINA1 gene","E":"CDKN2A gene"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Germline CDKN2A mutation causes FAMMM syndrome, with familial atypical moles, melanoma, and pancreatic cancer. BRCA2, PALB2, and PRSS1 are other pancreatic cancer genes without this melanoma pattern, and SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, germline predisposition, lines 3905–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 10 percent of patients carry germline mutations in cancer genes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists HNPCC, neurofibromatosis type 1, and hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome as inherited risks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has episodes of confusion during fasting that resolve when she eats. During an episode her glucose is 40 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), with high insulin and C peptide. Which histology is most likely in her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian type stroma","B":"Cords with amyloid","C":"Glycogen rich clear cells","D":"Signet ring cells","E":"Desmoplastic glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fasting hypoglycemia relieved by food with high insulin and C peptide is the Whipple triad of insulinoma, which shows monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with amylin derived amyloid. Ovarian type stroma marks mucinous cystic neoplasms, glycogen rich clear cells mark serous cystadenoma, desmoplastic glands mark ductal adenocarcinoma, and signet ring cells mark diffuse gastric cancer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinomas, lines 3911–3925); Robbins Ch. 19 (ductal adenocarcinoma desmoplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (diffuse gastric carcinoma signet ring cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp defines the Whipple triad as hypoglycemic symptoms, hypoglycemia during the episode, and relief with glucose (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma). COMLEX: congenital hyperinsulinism occurs in infants of diabetic mothers and Beckwith Wiedemann syndrome (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with MEN1 has severe, refractory ulcers in his duodenum and jejunum and chronic diarrhea. Where are his gastrinomas most likely found?","choices":{"A":"Single pancreatic tail mass","B":"Single pancreatic head mass","C":"Multiple duodenal tumors","D":"Gastric antral polyps","E":"Primary hepatic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MEN1 associated gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and located in the duodenum, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are typically single. Gastrinomas arise in the pancreas or the duodenal gastrinoma triangle, not as primary liver tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3938).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome comes from acid inactivation of pancreatic enzymes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists refractory peptic ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss in gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion by gastrin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea of 4 liters a day, potassium of 2.6 mEq/L (reference 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and achlorhydria. CT shows a large mass in his pancreatic tail. Which mechanism explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP secretion","B":"Excess gastrin secretion","C":"Excess serotonin secretion","D":"Excess glucagon secretion","E":"Excess calcitonin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A VIPoma, typically a large solitary tail tumor, causes WDHA or Verner Morrison syndrome: watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria. Gastrin excess raises acid, the opposite of achlorhydria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, VIPomas, lines 3948–3957).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes VIP driving bicarbonate and chloride secretion by intestinal epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss in VIPoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy whose mother had polyhydramnios vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of his duodenum. Which condition is classically associated with his anomaly?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Trisomy 21","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum and is associated with trisomy 21, or Down syndrome. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas (merged_with_ocr line 146); First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology (fa_gastro_cache line 191); ring anatomy from Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes annular pancreas may narrow the duodenum and cause vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a distended upper and scaphoid lower abdomen in annular pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease develops acute epigastric pain radiating to his back a few weeks after starting a new maintenance drug. He does not drink and has no gallstones. Which drug most likely caused his pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Azathioprine","B":"Amoxicillin","C":"Metformin","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Levothyroxine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lecture lists azathioprine, statins, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis, beside gallstones, alcohol, hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, trauma, ERCP, infections, and genetic mutations. Amoxicillin, metformin, acetaminophen, and levothyroxine are not among the drug causes it lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, etiology, lines 3763–3769).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mumps and coxsackievirus are infectious causes of acute pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists post ERCP injury among traumatic or iatrogenic causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has an incidental cystic mass in her pancreatic tail. Resection shows small cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin fluid. Which gross appearance fits this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Thick mucin cysts","B":"Honeycomb microcysts","C":"Main duct dilation","D":"Necrotic debris cavity","E":"Solid papillary mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas have a microcystic, honeycomb appearance with 1 to 3 mm cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin, straw colored fluid. Thick mucin fits MCN, main duct dilation fits IPMN, and a debris cavity fits a pseudocyst. A solid papillary mass fits the solid pseudopapillary neoplasm, which has solid and cystic components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3837–3848); Robbins Ch. 19 (solid pseudopapillary neoplasm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection of a serous cystadenoma is curative if it causes symptoms from mass effect (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs lack the dense ovarian stroma of MCNs (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a resected pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. Molecular testing shows the tumor has lost TGF beta signaling. Which gene is most likely inactivated?","choices":{"A":"KRAS","B":"TP53","C":"CDKN2A","D":"BRCA2","E":"SMAD4"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SMAD4 on 18q is inactivated in 55 percent of pancreatic adenocarcinomas at the PanIN 3 stage and abolishes TGF beta signaling. KRAS is activated rather than lost, TP53 loss impairs the DNA damage response, and CDKN2A loss disrupts the cell cycle. BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene, not the TGF beta pathway gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3904).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TP53 on 17p is inactivated in 70 to 75 percent of cases (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid lists chronic pancreatitis, especially beyond 20 years, as a risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman has mild diabetes, glossitis, anemia, a deep vein thrombosis, and a painful, migrating, blistering red rash. Which cells most likely gave rise to her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cells","B":"Islet delta cells","C":"Islet alpha cells","D":"Duodenal G cells","E":"Islet PP cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild diabetes, a migrating blistering rash, anemia, glossitis, and DVT in a postmenopausal woman fit a glucagonoma, an alpha cell tumor with extremely high plasma glucagon. Beta cells form insulinomas and G cells form gastrinomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas show monotonous cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and a high proportion are malignant (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes glucagonomas as arising from alpha cells with autonomous glucagon release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Beta cells form insulinomas an"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with decades of chronic pancreatitis has already developed steatorrhea, and now his fasting glucose begins to rise. Which complication is he developing?","choices":{"A":"Secondary diabetes","B":"Fat soluble vitamin loss","C":"Chronic steatorrhea","D":"Pseudocyst formation","E":"Protein azotorrhea"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys exocrine tissue first, while the islets are preserved until late, so secondary diabetes from endocrine failure develops after exocrine insufficiency. Vitamin loss, steatorrhea, and azotorrhea are exocrine, and pseudocysts are structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3798–3801, 3817–3822).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic pancreatitis raises the risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, up to 40 percent lifetime in PRSS1 hereditary pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: autoimmune type 1 pancreatitis shows storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis, and IgG4 positive plasma cells (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Vitamin loss, steatorrhea, and"}}],"20":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, below what level of normal enzyme secretion does exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occur?","choices":{"A":"Below 10 percent","B":"Below 50 percent","C":"Below 30 percent","D":"Below 75 percent","E":"Below 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when pancreatic enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. It is most often caused by cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis, or surgical resection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, etiology and pathophysiology of EPI, lines 3960–3963).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears at less than 10 percent pancreatic function (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists decreased fecal elastase 1 among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the source of the enzyme extracts used in pancrelipase?","choices":{"A":"Recombinant yeast","B":"Porcine pancreas","C":"Bovine stomach","D":"Fungal fermentation","E":"Human donor plasma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replacement therapy uses animal derived porcine pancreatic extracts containing lipase, amylase, and proteases. Pancrelipase gives about 12 times the lipolytic activity of older pancreatin extracts. Recombinant yeast, bovine stomach, fungal fermentation, and human donor plasma are not the source the lecture gives. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, therapeutic rationale, lines 3967–3972).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancrelipase gives more than 4 times the proteolytic activity per weight of pancreatin (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when should pancrelipase be taken?","choices":{"A":"Once daily at bedtime","B":"Before breakfast on waking","C":"With each meal and snack","D":"Twice daily between meals","E":"Weekly with a fatty meal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancrelipase is given orally with every meal and snack, with the dose individualized by body weight, severity of insufficiency, and dietary fat intake. Once daily, on waking, between meal, or weekly dosing would leave most meals without enzyme, so they do not match the every meal and snack schedule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3989).","boardPrep":"Step 1: therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cystic fibrosis among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Once daily, on waking, between","B":"Once daily, on waking, between","D":"Once daily, on waking, between","E":"Once daily, on waking, between"}},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptor does obeticholic acid activate?","choices":{"A":"Farnesoid X receptor","B":"Pregnane X receptor","C":"Liver X receptor alpha","D":"PPAR gamma receptor","E":"Vitamin D receptor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is a synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative and a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, or FXR, which downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism, lines 4029–4035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is given at 5 to 10 mg per day for PBC (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis (First Aid 2025 GI primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much of the circulating bile acid pool does long term ursodiol become?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"80 to 90 percent","D":"Nearly 100 percent","E":"30 to 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol is normally less than 5 percent of the bile salt pool, but daily long term dosing expands it to 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol pharmacokinetics, lines 4001–4007).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is conjugated with glycine or taurine and has a serum half life of about 100 hours (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which adverse effect of obeticholic acid can lead to stopping treatment?","choices":{"A":"Severe hepatotoxicity","B":"Uric acid kidney stones","C":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","D":"Dose dependent pruritus","E":"Fibrosing colonopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Obeticholic acid causes severe, dose dependent pruritus in up to 25 percent of patients, leading to stopping in up to 10 percent. Uric acid stones, mucositis, and colonopathy are pancrelipase effects, and hepatotoxicity is the problem the lecture ties to the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid adverse effects, lines 4043–4045; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid has been studied in MASLD, with improved liver fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholestyramine with ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with alcohol related chronic pancreatitis takes non enteric coated pancrelipase tablets with meals, but his greasy stools persist. Which drug should be added to improve his response?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine resin","B":"Loperamide with meals","C":"Metronidazole course","D":"Ondansetron before meals","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric acid rapidly and permanently inactivates pancrelipase, so non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace must be given with acid suppression, a PPI or H2 blocker. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, loperamide only slows transit, metronidazole is a nitroimidazole with potent activity against anaerobes, and ondansetron is a 5 HT3 antagonist antiemetic; none protects the enzymes from acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983); Katzung Ch 50, metronidazole, lines 22–27; Katzung Ch 62, serotonin 5 HT3 antagonists, lines 1802–1806.","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric coated capsules dissolve in alkaline duodenal pH, so they need no routine acid suppression (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic alcohol use and tobacco use as causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has taken high dose pancrelipase for years. She now has crampy abdominal pain and distension, and imaging shows a long stricture of her ascending colon. Which complication best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Meconium plug ileus","B":"Ileocolic intussusception","C":"Sigmoid colon volvulus","D":"Fibrosing colonopathy","E":"Toxic colonic dilation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy, with colonic strictures, in patients with cystic fibrosis. Meconium ileus affects newborns, volvulus and intussusception cause acute obstruction without a long stricture, and toxic dilation follows severe colitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excessive pancrelipase doses can cause diarrhea and abdominal pain (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists cystic fibrosis among genetic risk factors for chronic pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on high dose pancrelipase after a Whipple resection develops flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a renal stone that was not seen on plain film. Which property of his therapy best explains this?","choices":{"A":"High purine content","B":"High oxalate content","C":"High calcium content","D":"High cystine content","E":"High phosphate load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses can cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones, which are radiolucent, unlike radiopaque calcium stones such as calcium oxalate. Phosphate is not a constituent the lecture flags in the extracts, and cystine stones come from the inborn error cystinuria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996); Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt, lines 4907–4908 (cystinuria) and 4953 (radiolucent uric acid stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capsules must be swallowed whole, since chewing them can cause oropharyngeal mucositis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes chronic pancreatitis can cause diabetes mellitus from pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with symptomatic gallstones refuses surgery. Ultrasound shows several 4 mm stones, and plain film shows no calcified stones. Which mechanism explains how the drug chosen for her will dissolve them?","choices":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the gut","B":"Lower biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Activating hepatic FXR signaling","D":"Raising bilirubin conjugation rate","E":"Stimulating gallbladder contraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol dissolves small, radiolucent, noncalcified cholesterol stones by suppressing hepatic cholesterol secretion and lowering the cholesterol content of bile, with dissolution in up to 50 percent over 12 to 24 months. Sequestrants bind bile acids, and FXR agonism is obeticholic acid. Raising bilirubin conjugation and stimulating gallbladder contraction are not ursodiol actions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism and uses, lines 4008–4018).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol also prevents gallstones in obese patients during rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholesterol supersaturation of bile among causes of cholelithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sequestrants bind bile acids, "}},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with antimitochondrial antibodies has had a high alkaline phosphatase for a year despite full dose ursodiol. Which drug should be added?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Chenodeoxycholate","C":"Obeticholic acid","D":"Oral prednisone","E":"Oral pancrelipase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is approved for PBC at 5 to 10 mg per day with ursodiol when the response to ursodiol alone is inadequate, or alone if ursodiol is not tolerated. Chenodeoxycholate is hepatotoxic, and pancrelipase treats exocrine insufficiency. Cholestyramine, a bile acid binding resin, can ease cholestatic pruritus but does not treat the disease, and prednisone is not among the lecture's PBC drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid clinical uses, lines 4037–4042; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028); Katzung Ch. 35 (bile acid binding resins relieve pruritus in cholestasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day is first line for early PBC and delays transplant (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists liver transplant in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with primary biliary cholangitis stopped his first-line bile acid therapy after 3 months because he could not tolerate it. His serum alkaline phosphatase is 410 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L) and total bilirubin is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL). He starts once-daily oral monotherapy with a synthetic derivative of chenodeoxycholic acid. Which hepatic effect of this drug most directly protects his hepatocytes and cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Reduced bile acid synthesis","B":"Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Binding of bile acids in the gut","D":"Blocked ileal bile acid uptake","E":"Inhibited hepatic cholesterol synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative given as monotherapy for primary biliary cholangitis when the first-line agent is not tolerated is obeticholic acid. It is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, and FXR activation downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis; the lower intracellular concentration of toxic endogenous bile acids protects hepatocytes and cholangiocytes, and the drug also modulates inflammation and reduces fibrosis. Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion is the action of ursodiol, which lowers the cholesterol content of bile and dissolves small cholesterol stones. Binding bile acids in the gut lumen describes the nonabsorbed bile acid sequestrants, which do not act on the hepatocyte nucleus. Blocking ileal bile acid uptake interrupts enterohepatic recirculation in the gut rather than acting through a hepatic nuclear receptor. Inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis is the action of statins, not of a bile acid agent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism and clinical uses, lines 4029–4046; ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4013); First Aid 2025 GI (bile, rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI, bile). COMLEX: First Aid gives ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis, the setting for add-on or substitute bile acid agents (First Aid 2025 GI, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis takes enteric coated pancrelipase capsules, but he has been chewing them because they are hard to swallow. He now has painful sores in his mouth. Which adverse effect is he showing?","choices":{"A":"Herpetic gingivostomatitis","B":"Oral candidiasis","C":"Aphthous Crohn ulcers","D":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","E":"Vitamin B12 glossitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancrelipase capsules must be swallowed whole and not chewed, because contact with active enzymes can cause oropharyngeal mucositis. Herpetic gingivostomatitis, candidiasis, aphthous Crohn ulcers, and B12 glossitis are not tied to chewing the capsules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3993).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy in cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 is starting a very low calorie diet for rapid weight loss before bariatric surgery. Her physician wants to lower her risk of forming gallstones during this period. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Obeticholic acid","C":"Orlistat","D":"Gemfibrozil","E":"Ursodiol"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol prevents gallstone formation in obese patients undergoing rapid weight loss by lowering the cholesterol content of bile. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, fibrates raise gallstone risk, orlistat is a GI lipase inhibitor that reduces lipid absorption for weight loss rather than a stone preventive, and obeticholic acid is a second line PBC drug. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses, lines 4008–4020); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis; Katzung Ch 16, anti obesity drugs, lines 1781–1785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is first line for early PBC at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists obesity, female sex, fibrates, and rapid weight loss as gallstone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cholestyramine binds bile acid"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes delayed release pancrelipase capsules with meals and no acid suppression. Her steatorrhea is well controlled. Which feature of her capsules explains why no PPI is needed?","choices":{"A":"Coat dissolves at duodenal pH","B":"Acid stable form of lipase","C":"Gastric lipase is activated","D":"Bile salts shield the enzymes","E":"Jejunal uptake of enzymes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric coated, delayed release capsules such as Creon have acid resistant coatings that protect the enzymes from gastric acid and dissolve in the alkaline duodenum, so routine acid suppression is not needed. The enzymes themselves are inactivated by acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983).","boardPrep":"Step 1: non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace need a PPI or H2 blocker (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency causes steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The enzymes themselves are ina"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with primary biliary cholangitis starts ursodiol. Which mechanism best explains how it protects her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Binds bile acids in the gut lumen","B":"Blocks hepatic bile acid uptake","C":"Displaces hydrophobic bile acids","D":"Acts as a potent FXR agonist","E":"Raises cholesterol secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ursodiol stabilizes canalicular membranes and protects hepatocytes by replacing toxic hydrophobic bile acids with nontoxic hydrophilic ursodiol, with cytoprotective and anti inflammatory effects. It lowers rather than raises biliary cholesterol secretion, it does not bind gut bile acids or block their uptake, and potent FXR agonism is the action of obeticholic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4014).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is added for PBC with an inadequate response to ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes PBC as T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has steatorrhea and weight loss after a total pancreatectomy. Which starting lipase dose per meal, in USP units, is standard?","choices":{"A":"60,000 to 90,000 units","B":"9,000 to 12,000 units","C":"5,000 to 8,000 units","D":"120,000 to 180,000 units","E":"2,000 to 4,000 units"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancrelipase therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units, or 20,000 to 30,000 IU, of lipase per meal, which reduces steatorrhea to insignificant levels in most patients. Excess doses cause diarrhea and pain. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical pancreatic resection is a common cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a positive Sudan III stain among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has symptomatic small radiolucent gallstones and is not a surgical candidate. His physician chooses ursodiol over the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Which advantage supports this choice?","choices":{"A":"Faster dissolution","B":"Lacks hepatotoxicity","C":"Clears calcified stones","D":"Once weekly dosing","E":"Clears pigment stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol is remarkably safe and virtually free of serious toxicity, and unlike chenodeoxycholate it is not hepatotoxic. It works only on small, noncalcified cholesterol stones and takes 12 to 24 months. Dissolution is not faster, since it takes 12 to 24 months, and dosing is daily, not weekly. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses and adverse effects, lines 4015–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salt induced diarrhea is uncommon with ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are generally radiolucent on X ray (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"It works only on small, noncal","E":"It works only on small, noncal"}}]};
